00:01:02.849 --> 00:01:03.829
Startups?

00:01:03.829 --> 00:01:04.959
(laughter)

00:01:04.959 --> 00:01:08.277
Okay, this is important.
Is anyone looking for a job?

00:01:08.277 --> 00:01:09.509
(laughter)

00:01:09.509 --> 00:01:10.891
This guy right here. Okay.

00:01:10.891 --> 00:01:13.589
Talk to all those people around you.
They're hiring.

00:01:13.589 --> 00:01:15.075
So we'll get you set up.

00:01:15.075 --> 00:01:18.507
Look, it's a great time to be a developer.

00:01:18.507 --> 00:01:20.944
Every industry is changing, right?

00:01:20.944 --> 00:01:24.034
Software is right in the middle
of a lot of those changes.

00:01:24.034 --> 00:01:26.656
And you, the developers,
you're right in the heart of it.

00:01:26.663 --> 00:01:29.051
You're driving all that.

00:01:29.051 --> 00:01:30.815
And think about the tools that you use.

00:01:30.818 --> 00:01:33.789
The tools that we use
are changing every day also.

00:01:33.798 --> 00:01:37.055
Open source has been just a huge part
of that transformation.

00:01:37.055 --> 00:01:38.055
Think about it.

00:01:38.058 --> 00:01:41.851
When's the last time
you actually set up a server?

00:01:41.851 --> 00:01:43.452
That's an API call now.

00:01:43.456 --> 00:01:47.102
How about configured
an operating system image?

00:01:47.109 --> 00:01:49.233
You don't do that, right?
That's all containers.

00:01:49.235 --> 00:01:51.709
It's all just a library
of container images now.

00:01:51.715 --> 00:01:53.380
How about set up a whole fleet?

00:01:53.380 --> 00:01:55.892
Do you set up a whole fleet,
or do you Terraform it?

00:01:55.892 --> 00:01:57.988
Right? That's the way it works now.

00:01:57.989 --> 00:02:00.540
So the other thing that I think
is a really interesting change

00:02:00.540 --> 00:02:03.963
is the very role of a developer is changing.

00:02:03.973 --> 00:02:06.035
You don't just write code.

00:02:06.035 --> 00:02:07.813
You're working with big data.

00:02:07.813 --> 00:02:10.548
You're working with event streams
and IoT data.

00:02:10.552 --> 00:02:13.473
You're starting to do data science
and machine learning.

00:02:13.473 --> 00:02:16.457
Who's written their first
machine learning app so far?

00:02:16.457 --> 00:02:18.189
Yeah, it's just emerging.

00:02:18.193 --> 00:02:22.066
It's super exciting to help you all
go on this journey together.

00:02:22.069 --> 00:02:25.132
You're getting to know your users,
you're doing user experience.

00:02:25.138 --> 00:02:28.297
You're managing <i>products</i>, not just <i>projects</i>.

00:02:28.297 --> 00:02:30.661
And, ultimately, you're working as a team

00:02:30.661 --> 00:02:34.361
to ship great experiences
out to users and customers.

00:02:34.361 --> 00:02:36.447
And I think
that's a pretty amazing change.

00:02:36.447 --> 00:02:40.187
Now, cloud is enabling all this.

00:02:40.187 --> 00:02:44.502
We're all sort of on this journey
to evolve together to this new world.

00:02:44.502 --> 00:02:47.531
We all have access
to this amazing technology.

00:02:47.531 --> 00:02:51.054
You can just get it as a service.
Everything is available as a service.

00:02:51.054 --> 00:02:54.985
So I'm pretty sure the next five years
are going to be even more exciting

00:02:54.985 --> 00:02:56.776
than the last five years.

00:02:57.649 --> 00:03:00.852
To tell that story, we wanted to talk
to some community members,

00:03:00.861 --> 00:03:02.589
get some voices from our community.

00:03:02.597 --> 00:03:04.514
Any community members here in the room

00:03:04.514 --> 00:03:07.150
from the Google developer communities
out around the world?

00:03:07.150 --> 00:03:08.435
Right on!
(loud cheering)

00:03:08.435 --> 00:03:11.568
Okay, we made a little video
to show you some of their stories

00:03:11.568 --> 00:03:13.257
and some of the things they're working on.

00:03:13.257 --> 00:03:15.987
So with that, I'm going
to roll a little video for you.

00:03:16.797 --> 00:03:19.523
♪ (music) ♪

00:03:20.092 --> 00:03:22.981
Funny enough,
we started with a simple conversation.

00:03:24.382 --> 00:03:26.344
<i>So, imagine it's election day.</i>

00:03:26.344 --> 00:03:30.287
Thirty, forty million people
showing up to vote.

00:03:30.831 --> 00:03:34.432
What our software does is allow them
to register these people,

00:03:34.432 --> 00:03:38.578
check for duplicates
or if there are any signs of fraud.

00:03:40.618 --> 00:03:42.058
<i>(Akanksha) We have a loyalty program</i>

00:03:42.058 --> 00:03:44.758
where an average person can earn points

00:03:44.758 --> 00:03:46.768
every time they shop for groceries,

00:03:46.775 --> 00:03:50.243
and this currency effectively becomes
a second source of wealth

00:03:50.243 --> 00:03:52.751
to pay for everything
from your electricity bill

00:03:52.751 --> 00:03:54.862
<i>to school fees and doctors' bills.</i>

00:03:56.279 --> 00:04:00.303
Two of my cousin's sisters
suffered from breast cancer,

00:04:00.303 --> 00:04:02.575
and I ended up losing them.

00:04:02.575 --> 00:04:06.375
<i>That's when I thought,
"We need to do something with technology,</i>

00:04:06.375 --> 00:04:09.687
to build a new way
of doing breast cancer screening.

00:04:10.253 --> 00:04:11.660
<i>(Piyali Dey) In my graduate school,</i>

00:04:11.660 --> 00:04:15.513
<i>I was one of two women in a class of 45.</i>

00:04:15.513 --> 00:04:17.868
And it kind of got me thinking

00:04:17.868 --> 00:04:20.252
that this is not what it's supposed to be.

00:04:20.911 --> 00:04:23.556
<i>(Rafael Figueroa) We have
this patient called Maria.</i>

00:04:23.556 --> 00:04:25.113
<i>She is six years old.</i>

00:04:25.113 --> 00:04:27.333
And she was having these daily seizures.

00:04:27.333 --> 00:04:30.840
She lived in a remote area in Brazil
where there are no specialists.

00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:33.003
<i>And no one in the city could help her.</i>

00:04:34.053 --> 00:04:36.133
<i>(Amanda Cavallaro) I'm a developer
during the day,</i>

00:04:36.133 --> 00:04:40.173
But then during the evening
I wear my cape, and I do Google stuff.

00:04:42.341 --> 00:04:44.143
<i>(Femi) One of the things that is most amazing</i>

00:04:44.143 --> 00:04:46.576
is being on Google Cloud has enabled us

00:04:46.576 --> 00:04:50.598
to be online and available 100% of the time

00:04:50.598 --> 00:04:54.539
<i>with automated health checks,
security checks, and logs monitoring.</i>

00:04:54.987 --> 00:04:59.160
I use tools like Stackdriver
to keep me on top of all of that.

00:04:59.171 --> 00:05:01.112
<i>(Akanksha) Google and the Android platform</i>

00:05:01.112 --> 00:05:05.001
have become kind of a backbone
of our entire product ecosystem,

00:05:05.001 --> 00:05:08.369
<i>where we could actually
build this loyalty program</i>

00:05:08.369 --> 00:05:10.238
powered by mobile phones.

00:05:10.238 --> 00:05:11.429
We use deep learning

00:05:11.429 --> 00:05:13.957
and the technology stack called TensorFlow

00:05:13.957 --> 00:05:16.955
<i>to interpret thermography,</i>

00:05:16.955 --> 00:05:19.645
which basically measures
the temperature on the chest

00:05:19.645 --> 00:05:23.452
and then figures out if there is
any abnormal tissue activity.

00:05:23.452 --> 00:05:27.616
<i>And this can be five to eight years
before a lump is formed or felt.</i>

00:05:27.616 --> 00:05:30.272
We're actually helping people
to get access to doctors

00:05:30.272 --> 00:05:32.028
that they couldn't have without the stack.

00:05:32.028 --> 00:05:33.920
<i>For example, this case with Maria--</i>

00:05:33.920 --> 00:05:36.461
<i>with Google Cloud,
we connect to the clinic device,</i>

00:05:36.461 --> 00:05:38.614
measure Maria's brainwaves,

00:05:38.614 --> 00:05:41.372
send to the best
child neurologist in the country,

00:05:41.372 --> 00:05:43.519
<i>3,000 kilometers away, in São Paulo,</i>

00:05:43.519 --> 00:05:45.045
<i>and she found a cure for Maria.</i>

00:05:45.045 --> 00:05:46.898
And this was awesome.

00:05:46.898 --> 00:05:49.519
I run the Google Developer Group,
Vancouver chapter.

00:05:49.519 --> 00:05:51.579
<i>I see girls from high school.</i>

00:05:51.579 --> 00:05:53.543
<i>They actually show up at our meetups.</i>

00:05:53.543 --> 00:05:55.180
<i>They know nothing about the product,</i>

00:05:55.180 --> 00:05:58.040
<i>and after like one hour,
or two hours of time,</i>

00:05:58.040 --> 00:06:00.609
<i>they can build something from scratch.</i>

00:06:00.609 --> 00:06:03.514
<i>(Amanda) I'm one of the co-organizers
of GDG Cloud, London.</i>

00:06:03.514 --> 00:06:06.854
<i>We are the most active
GDG Cloud community in the world,</i>

00:06:06.854 --> 00:06:11.203
and I love how you can achieve
all these dreams and passions

00:06:11.203 --> 00:06:14.185
and ideas you have
using the Google Cloud platform.

00:06:14.985 --> 00:06:19.311
Whatever you do in life,
try to solve a meaningful problem.

00:06:19.311 --> 00:06:21.998
More than ever,
people are asking for a purpose

00:06:21.998 --> 00:06:23.352
in the work that they do.

00:06:23.352 --> 00:06:24.672
You're not just building product,

00:06:24.672 --> 00:06:27.263
you're building product
that can change people's lives.

00:06:27.263 --> 00:06:31.279
My hope is that we will excel in technology,

00:06:31.749 --> 00:06:34.154
women will get their seats at the table.

00:06:34.154 --> 00:06:36.337
I feel that Google just empowered me.

00:06:36.337 --> 00:06:39.966
&gt;From that moment, it's been love.
It's pure love.

00:06:39.966 --> 00:06:41.247
♪ (music) ♪

00:06:42.107 --> 00:06:43.867
(cheers and applause)

00:06:43.867 --> 00:06:45.168
So cool!

00:06:45.168 --> 00:06:46.101
Thank you.

00:06:46.101 --> 00:06:49.083
Thank you Amanda, and Femi,
and all the community members.

00:06:49.083 --> 00:06:50.763
So great to hear your stories.

00:06:50.763 --> 00:06:52.320
So look, we're going to kick off today.

00:06:52.320 --> 00:06:53.605
We're going to have a great day.

00:06:53.605 --> 00:06:55.801
But I really wanted to start
with a promise to you,

00:06:55.801 --> 00:06:59.291
a promise that Google is going
to really do three things.

00:06:59.291 --> 00:07:02.908
The first is continue
to deliver amazing innovation--

00:07:02.908 --> 00:07:06.527
things like AutoML and TPUs,

00:07:06.527 --> 00:07:09.446
silicon for TensorFlow at massive scale.

00:07:09.446 --> 00:07:12.642
The second thing we're going to do
is we're committed to open source

00:07:12.642 --> 00:07:13.726
and an open cloud.

00:07:13.726 --> 00:07:16.277
You know, Google's got
about 20 years of experience

00:07:16.277 --> 00:07:18.785
doing open source and being part
of the open source community

00:07:18.785 --> 00:07:20.907
under our belts, and we're looking forward

00:07:20.907 --> 00:07:23.004
to doing the next 20 years with you.

00:07:23.004 --> 00:07:24.923
And then the third thing
is that community theme,

00:07:24.923 --> 00:07:27.485
and we wanted to really launch that
at this conference.

00:07:27.485 --> 00:07:30.058
We believe in being part
of the developer community.

00:07:30.058 --> 00:07:31.688
We are engineers at Google.

00:07:31.688 --> 00:07:34.105
We sit all over the world with all of you.

00:07:34.105 --> 00:07:36.266
And we want to continue
to be really connected with you

00:07:36.266 --> 00:07:39.683
as we figure out
this next generation of technology.

00:07:39.688 --> 00:07:43.747
So, for the rest of the morning
we have one simple rule,

00:07:43.747 --> 00:07:45.694
and that is: "Show, don't tell."

00:07:45.694 --> 00:07:48.093
We've got a lot of great code.
It's going to be a lot of fun.

00:07:48.093 --> 00:07:50.846
And let's get started, yeah? Right on!

00:07:51.131 --> 00:07:54.577
I want to introduce
a great executive at Google,

00:07:54.577 --> 00:07:56.665
a Vice-President of Engineering,
Melody Meckfessel,

00:07:56.665 --> 00:07:58.199
is going to talk to you about tools.

00:07:58.199 --> 00:07:59.122
Thanks!

00:07:59.124 --> 00:08:00.698
(applause)

00:08:00.698 --> 00:08:03.119
♪ (music) ♪

00:08:11.376 --> 00:08:12.932
Thanks so much, Adam.

00:08:12.932 --> 00:08:15.662
I'm so happy to be here today.

00:08:15.662 --> 00:08:18.462
My name's Melody Meckfessel,
and I'm passionate about developers

00:08:18.462 --> 00:08:20.486
because I am one.

00:08:20.486 --> 00:08:24.409
And I also worked
for many years in operations,

00:08:24.409 --> 00:08:25.940
where I carried a pager.

00:08:25.940 --> 00:08:29.097
And I became very familiar with the phrase,

00:08:29.097 --> 00:08:31.326
"It works well on my machine."

00:08:31.326 --> 00:08:35.180
So I lead developer
and operator tools at Google.

00:08:35.180 --> 00:08:40.095
This includes Google engineers,
Chrome and Open Source developers,

00:08:40.095 --> 00:08:44.788
Android developers, and developers
using Google's Cloud Platform.

00:08:44.788 --> 00:08:49.262
Now, each one of us probably has
a different definition of DevOps.

00:08:49.262 --> 00:08:54.097
At Google, our understanding
is that DevOps includes two things:

00:08:54.097 --> 00:08:56.724
end-to-end automated tooling--

00:08:56.724 --> 00:08:59.458
from source, to build-and-test,

00:08:59.458 --> 00:09:02.501
to releasing with integrated observability--

00:09:02.501 --> 00:09:06.250
and the culture that supports
and enables developers.

00:09:06.836 --> 00:09:09.225
DevOps is near and dear to my heart,

00:09:09.225 --> 00:09:12.236
because it's about automating away the toil

00:09:12.236 --> 00:09:14.408
while empowering the humans.

00:09:14.408 --> 00:09:16.180
It makes us productive,

00:09:16.180 --> 00:09:19.302
and maybe just a little bit happy.

00:09:19.302 --> 00:09:21.633
So how many humans in the room today?

00:09:21.633 --> 00:09:22.871
(cheers)

00:09:22.871 --> 00:09:23.844
Yeah?

00:09:23.844 --> 00:09:25.937
(laughs) I hope so!

00:09:25.937 --> 00:09:30.082
Well, humans develop software
and operate services.

00:09:30.082 --> 00:09:32.390
And at Google, we've spent
a significant effort

00:09:32.390 --> 00:09:35.130
understanding productivity.

00:09:35.130 --> 00:09:39.494
Different developers need
different levels of insight and care.

00:09:39.871 --> 00:09:41.259
And, as developers,

00:09:41.259 --> 00:09:45.640
we want to choose the best DevOps tools
to get our work done.

00:09:46.112 --> 00:09:48.732
But that choice can be hard sometimes.

00:09:48.732 --> 00:09:50.322
Imagine being a new developer

00:09:50.322 --> 00:09:54.323
and having to pick your language
or framework that you need to use.

00:09:54.323 --> 00:09:59.474
Or where to deploy your app--
VM, Kubernetes.

00:09:59.474 --> 00:10:02.238
Or which cloud platform to use.

00:10:02.238 --> 00:10:05.457
Well, that one should be easy, right?
Google Cloud Platform.

00:10:05.457 --> 00:10:07.375
(chuckles) That's why we're here!

00:10:07.375 --> 00:10:10.365
And with so many choices at every stage,

00:10:10.365 --> 00:10:13.082
selecting the right DevOps tools
can be overwhelming

00:10:13.082 --> 00:10:14.788
and waste our time.

00:10:14.788 --> 00:10:19.190
At Google, our goal is to make
these DevOps tooling decisions

00:10:19.190 --> 00:10:20.951
easier for you.

00:10:20.951 --> 00:10:25.664
That way you can go back
to doing what you love--writing code,

00:10:25.664 --> 00:10:28.126
and bringing your idea to life quickly.

00:10:28.743 --> 00:10:30.439
But moving fast shouldn't come

00:10:30.439 --> 00:10:32.957
at the expense of security or stability,

00:10:32.957 --> 00:10:36.944
because without that,
any speed improvements are useless.

00:10:37.576 --> 00:10:41.887
Industry research shows
that high-performing DevOps teams

00:10:41.887 --> 00:10:46.251
achieve both faster releases
and better stability.

00:10:46.251 --> 00:10:50.035
They deploy 46 times more frequently

00:10:50.035 --> 00:10:52.516
than their low-performing peers.

00:10:53.377 --> 00:10:57.819
And their production deployments
are seven times less likely

00:10:57.819 --> 00:10:59.810
to result in a degraded service.

00:10:59.810 --> 00:11:01.354
So think about that.

00:11:01.354 --> 00:11:04.129
You get faster releases
with better stability.

00:11:04.756 --> 00:11:06.858
And what explains this behavior?

00:11:06.858 --> 00:11:10.790
Well, it comes back to this combination
of end-to-end tooling

00:11:10.790 --> 00:11:12.975
and the DevOps culture.

00:11:13.871 --> 00:11:19.394
At Google we've embraced and adopted
DevOps cultural principles.

00:11:19.394 --> 00:11:22.786
First, use data to guide your decisions.

00:11:22.786 --> 00:11:25.973
Dig into the code,
look at logs, look at traces,

00:11:25.973 --> 00:11:28.165
to understand what's going on.

00:11:28.165 --> 00:11:31.951
Have a shared fate and collaboration
between the humans--

00:11:31.951 --> 00:11:33.407
the developers and operators--

00:11:33.407 --> 00:11:36.377
because we know we're more powerful
when we work together.

00:11:37.083 --> 00:11:41.114
Choose transparency over secrecy and siloing.

00:11:41.114 --> 00:11:43.973
Everyone sees the same data and information

00:11:43.973 --> 00:11:47.453
by integrating observability into your apps.

00:11:47.453 --> 00:11:50.914
And, finally, embrace blameless postmortems

00:11:50.914 --> 00:11:54.582
to encourage continuous improvement
within your teams.

00:11:54.582 --> 00:11:57.155
We have to accept and learn from our failure.

00:11:57.155 --> 00:11:59.215
I mean, this is software development, right?

00:11:59.215 --> 00:12:01.265
We're going to have bugs.

00:12:01.265 --> 00:12:04.065
So all of this is super easy to say,

00:12:04.065 --> 00:12:06.799
but when you live it, it's magic.

00:12:06.799 --> 00:12:11.549
This is how we build trust
between developers and operators.

00:12:11.549 --> 00:12:15.306
We have to trust each other
to make it happen for our users.

00:12:15.306 --> 00:12:19.035
So I want to show you, today,
end-to-end DevOps tooling

00:12:19.035 --> 00:12:23.435
to help you build, deploy,
and monitor your apps

00:12:23.435 --> 00:12:25.549
quickly and safely.

00:12:25.549 --> 00:12:29.726
Our DevOps tools consist
of Cloud Source Repositories--

00:12:29.726 --> 00:12:33.869
a single place to store,
manage, and track your code;

00:12:33.869 --> 00:12:36.608
Cloud Build, a fully managed

00:12:36.608 --> 00:12:39.409
continuous integration and delivery platform

00:12:39.409 --> 00:12:42.895
that lets you build and test
your applications in the cloud

00:12:42.895 --> 00:12:44.919
at any scale;

00:12:44.919 --> 00:12:49.520
and Stackdriver--management tools
for developers and operators.

00:12:49.520 --> 00:12:51.732
So let's walk through a day in the life

00:12:51.732 --> 00:12:54.038
of a software developer and operator.

00:12:54.038 --> 00:12:56.693
We're going to build
an end-to-end automated pipeline

00:12:56.693 --> 00:12:58.888
using GCP DevOps tools

00:12:58.888 --> 00:13:03.067
with well-known open source tools
like GitHub and Spinnaker.

00:13:03.612 --> 00:13:06.750
We're going to go from finding
a problem in Stackdriver

00:13:06.750 --> 00:13:10.338
to using code search functionality
in Cloud Source Repositories

00:13:10.338 --> 00:13:11.735
to find the code.

00:13:11.735 --> 00:13:13.732
We're going to fix the code.

00:13:13.732 --> 00:13:16.323
We're going to commit the change to GitHub.

00:13:16.323 --> 00:13:19.546
We're going to kick off a build
using Cloud Build.

00:13:19.546 --> 00:13:22.695
And then we're going to deploy
to GKE using Spinnaker.

00:13:22.695 --> 00:13:24.705
And we're going to do all that
in just 10 minutes.

00:13:26.152 --> 00:13:27.650
- Morgan?
- (Morgan) Melody?

00:13:27.650 --> 00:13:28.812
Awesome to see you!

00:13:28.812 --> 00:13:31.179
(Morgan) Great to see you here.
Hello everybody.

00:13:31.179 --> 00:13:34.018
(Melody) Alright! So let's get started!

00:13:34.018 --> 00:13:35.571
(applause)

00:13:35.571 --> 00:13:37.115
So this is Hipstershop.

00:13:37.115 --> 00:13:39.166
You may remember it from Tuesday's keynote.

00:13:39.166 --> 00:13:41.230
We're going to purchase an item.

00:13:41.230 --> 00:13:43.386
(Morgan) And it is a lot slower
than it was on Tuesday.

00:13:43.386 --> 00:13:46.828
Yeah, I think it's popularity increased
over the last two days.

00:13:47.438 --> 00:13:49.226
So we're going to start our investigation

00:13:49.226 --> 00:13:53.443
looking at Stackdriver Trace,
which is part of our APM Suite.

00:13:53.443 --> 00:13:57.462
Our APM Suite lets you do
production debugging, profiling,

00:13:57.462 --> 00:14:00.516
and tracing on applications running anywhere.

00:14:00.516 --> 00:14:03.326
And with Google Ed projects like OpenCensus

00:14:03.326 --> 00:14:05.178
we're making it incredibly easy

00:14:05.178 --> 00:14:08.201
to extract telemetry from your applications,

00:14:08.201 --> 00:14:10.214
whatever tools you decide to use.

00:14:11.064 --> 00:14:14.781
So after looking at some traces
we see a couple of things.

00:14:14.781 --> 00:14:19.637
First, for a single customer request,
we see many child requests.

00:14:19.637 --> 00:14:21.177
So we're just going to batch those up.

00:14:21.177 --> 00:14:23.182
It's going to make it more efficient.

00:14:23.182 --> 00:14:25.906
But the second thing that we notice here

00:14:25.906 --> 00:14:28.001
is that the requests to the catalog service

00:14:28.001 --> 00:14:31.935
are incredibly...
... Whoa! Very slow!

00:14:31.935 --> 00:14:34.691
(Morgan) This one's about 2.6 seconds,
just for one of them.

00:14:34.691 --> 00:14:37.245
Okay, well we're going
to have to dig in on this.

00:14:37.245 --> 00:14:38.968
So we're going to go from Stackdriver Trace

00:14:38.968 --> 00:14:41.125
to Stackdriver Profiler,

00:14:41.125 --> 00:14:43.745
which profiles applications continually

00:14:43.745 --> 00:14:45.844
with no appreciable overhead.

00:14:45.844 --> 00:14:48.238
We've been using Profiler
at Google for many years,

00:14:48.238 --> 00:14:51.829
and now our customers get to use
the same functionality.

00:14:51.829 --> 00:14:53.944
So this icicle graph,

00:14:53.944 --> 00:14:56.865
it shows you the resources consumed

00:14:56.865 --> 00:14:59.068
by the function on the horizontal axis

00:14:59.068 --> 00:15:01.523
and the call hierarchy on the vertical axis.

00:15:01.523 --> 00:15:03.020
So because things are slow,

00:15:03.020 --> 00:15:05.714
we're going to go check out CPU time here.

00:15:07.415 --> 00:15:10.773
And what we can see, looking at Profiler,

00:15:10.773 --> 00:15:14.000
is that there's a really slow
GRPC <i>GetProduct</i> method,

00:15:14.000 --> 00:15:18.402
which is spending all of its time
waiting on this function, <i>parseCatalog</i>.

00:15:18.402 --> 00:15:20.392
I think it's taking...
... What is it now?

00:15:20.392 --> 00:15:22.383
- (Morgan) It's 92%.
- 92%?

00:15:22.383 --> 00:15:23.607
Yeah, it's bad.

00:15:23.607 --> 00:15:24.712
- That's bad.
- Yeah.

00:15:24.712 --> 00:15:26.520
That's very bad.

00:15:26.520 --> 00:15:29.834
So let's go look at the code
for <i>parseCatalog</i>.

00:15:30.450 --> 00:15:33.807
So this application code
is actually hosted on GitHub,

00:15:33.807 --> 00:15:36.994
and it's continuously mirrored
to Cloud Source Repositories

00:15:36.994 --> 00:15:39.078
where you can use functionality

00:15:39.078 --> 00:15:41.450
like powerful code search and indexing.

00:15:41.450 --> 00:15:43.694
So we're going to come here
to Cloud Source Repositories.

00:15:43.694 --> 00:15:46.632
We're going to search
for that function we saw on Profiler.

00:15:46.632 --> 00:15:47.951
And there it is!

00:15:48.681 --> 00:15:49.828
(Morgan) Real quick.

00:15:50.128 --> 00:15:52.351
(Melody) So let's take a look
at the blame layer.

00:15:52.851 --> 00:15:54.295
Wait... 
... Morgan?

00:15:54.295 --> 00:15:55.380
Morgan?

00:15:55.380 --> 00:15:56.868
(Morgan) You told me to slow it down.

00:15:56.868 --> 00:15:59.931
(laughing) He's responsible for this one.

00:15:59.931 --> 00:16:02.006
So we're going to hop to GitHub

00:16:03.083 --> 00:16:07.681
and check out that there's a pull request
that's been created

00:16:07.681 --> 00:16:09.868
that fixes these performance issues.

00:16:12.549 --> 00:16:14.078
And it's for Morgan.

00:16:14.078 --> 00:16:16.530
(Morgan) Yep. Got to fix my own bugs.

00:16:16.530 --> 00:16:18.086
(Melody) Amazing.

00:16:18.086 --> 00:16:19.793
(laughter)

00:16:19.793 --> 00:16:21.462
(Melody) So I'm going to approve

00:16:21.462 --> 00:16:23.081
the pull request from Morgan,

00:16:23.081 --> 00:16:27.632
and this is going to automatically trigger
a Cloud Build run,

00:16:28.289 --> 00:16:30.959
which you can see in the Cloud Build UI.

00:16:34.237 --> 00:16:37.051
Alright! There it is!

00:16:37.374 --> 00:16:41.767
So Zendesk, Lyft, and over 1,000 customers
use Cloud Build daily

00:16:41.767 --> 00:16:43.285
to ship new functionality.

00:16:43.867 --> 00:16:46.393
So, as a reminder, we're doing this all live.

00:16:46.393 --> 00:16:48.237
The build's going to take a couple minutes,

00:16:48.237 --> 00:16:51.719
so let's cross our fingers on this one.

00:16:51.719 --> 00:16:55.985
At Google we create over 4 million builds
in the cloud every day

00:16:55.985 --> 00:16:59.603
because it's flexible,
it's secure, and it scales.

00:17:00.324 --> 00:17:02.655
But right now let's go back
and look at the pull request

00:17:02.655 --> 00:17:04.469
that we just approved.

00:17:05.519 --> 00:17:08.548
We can see that Cloud Build
also ran a test build

00:17:08.548 --> 00:17:10.597
when the pull request was created.

00:17:10.597 --> 00:17:12.480
And we did this
because we wanted to make sure

00:17:12.480 --> 00:17:14.901
that the pull request wasn't broken.

00:17:16.026 --> 00:17:17.704
Now, all of this is being done

00:17:17.704 --> 00:17:20.812
through our new
Cloud Build-GitHub partnership

00:17:20.812 --> 00:17:23.639
which we are excited to announce today.

00:17:24.321 --> 00:17:27.230
With this partnership,
developers are able to use

00:17:27.230 --> 00:17:32.656
Cloud Build CI capabilities
to validate changes, run tests,

00:17:32.656 --> 00:17:35.857
and get results, right within the GitHub UI,

00:17:35.857 --> 00:17:37.771
in a frictionless way.

00:17:38.555 --> 00:17:41.192
So it's super easy to get started.

00:17:41.192 --> 00:17:43.099
As long as you have a GitHub repo

00:17:43.099 --> 00:17:45.988
with a <i>Dockerfile</i> in the root directory,

00:17:45.988 --> 00:17:49.718
GitHub will automatically suggest Cloud Build

00:17:49.718 --> 00:17:51.424
and its CI capabilities.

00:17:51.424 --> 00:17:52.916
You can see it here.

00:17:55.586 --> 00:17:58.959
And you can get started running builds
in just seconds.

00:17:59.891 --> 00:18:02.655
Cloud Build makes
high-velocity software development

00:18:02.655 --> 00:18:04.740
safe, easy, and secure.

00:18:06.362 --> 00:18:09.062
It really unleashes your team's productivity.

00:18:09.062 --> 00:18:13.767
So let's check the build. It's almost done.

00:18:13.767 --> 00:18:14.934
(Morgan) Almost done.

00:18:14.934 --> 00:18:16.290
(Melody) Hopefully.

00:18:18.529 --> 00:18:21.307
This is where we need the DJ
to be playing a little bit of music.

00:18:21.317 --> 00:18:22.914
One of the nice things about Cloud Build

00:18:22.914 --> 00:18:24.711
is it shows you the logs
right in the console,

00:18:24.711 --> 00:18:26.088
which you can see here.

00:18:26.088 --> 00:18:27.952
And so it's finishing
the final step right now.

00:18:27.952 --> 00:18:30.225
It's pushing it up to GCR,
and now we're done.

00:18:30.225 --> 00:18:33.423
Alright! Live demo, folks!

00:18:33.423 --> 00:18:34.738
(cheers)

00:18:34.738 --> 00:18:37.394
(Melody laughs)

00:18:37.854 --> 00:18:41.212
So all the tests passed. We're good to go.

00:18:41.212 --> 00:18:42.930
But I keep mentioning security.

00:18:42.930 --> 00:18:44.422
So I want to show you,

00:18:44.422 --> 00:18:47.907
Cloud Build will actually
do security scanning for you.

00:18:48.371 --> 00:18:49.808
So we're going to kick off a scan.

00:18:49.808 --> 00:18:52.001
And actually it's so fast...

00:18:52.001 --> 00:18:55.097
... We didn't find any vulnerabilities,
so we're good to deploy that.

00:18:55.097 --> 00:18:56.696
And Morgan, if you can show an example

00:18:56.696 --> 00:18:58.515
of maybe a past project with some...

00:18:58.515 --> 00:19:00.674
... So you can see here
we wouldn't deploy these images

00:19:00.674 --> 00:19:03.478
because we need to fix the issues first.

00:19:03.478 --> 00:19:05.862
Alright, so we've done our build,
we've done our tests,

00:19:05.862 --> 00:19:07.613
we've checked for vulnerabilities.

00:19:07.613 --> 00:19:10.519
Cloud Build is now going to trigger
a Spinnaker pipeline

00:19:10.519 --> 00:19:12.212
to deploy the image.

00:19:12.212 --> 00:19:13.809
Google has been a core contributor

00:19:13.809 --> 00:19:16.280
to the Spinnaker open source project.

00:19:16.847 --> 00:19:21.417
This was created to make
multi-cloud deployments safe,

00:19:21.417 --> 00:19:23.376
fast, and secure.

00:19:23.981 --> 00:19:27.209
So today we're going to see
automated canary analysis,

00:19:27.209 --> 00:19:31.398
which reduces the risk associated
with deploying to production.

00:19:31.398 --> 00:19:34.611
So while we're performing
the automated canary analysis

00:19:34.611 --> 00:19:36.451
we're going to automatically promote things,

00:19:36.451 --> 00:19:38.653
because we don't have a lot of time today.

00:19:38.653 --> 00:19:40.060
But what you're going to see...

00:19:40.060 --> 00:19:42.369
... This is an actual pipeline that's running

00:19:42.369 --> 00:19:44.294
and it has three stages.

00:19:44.294 --> 00:19:46.753
In the first stage,
the canary build is pushed

00:19:46.753 --> 00:19:48.673
to a single instance of the app

00:19:48.673 --> 00:19:51.043
and some traffic is routed to it.

00:19:51.043 --> 00:19:52.361
In the second phase,

00:19:52.361 --> 00:19:54.745
we would normally check things like metrics,

00:19:54.745 --> 00:19:56.545
to see how it's developing,

00:19:56.545 --> 00:19:59.158
but we've kind of automatically
pushed this forward

00:19:59.158 --> 00:20:02.526
because it would take about 30 minutes
for us to do this task.

00:20:03.211 --> 00:20:06.863
And in the third stage
the build will promote the canary

00:20:06.863 --> 00:20:09.981
to all the remaining production instances.

00:20:09.981 --> 00:20:12.809
And as you can see, the different stages--

00:20:12.809 --> 00:20:16.751
the color turns from blue to green
as it progresses

00:20:16.751 --> 00:20:18.825
through the canary analysis.

00:20:19.429 --> 00:20:25.456
So I'm sure everyone here
is familiar with canary analysis itself,

00:20:25.456 --> 00:20:28.983
but quite often when we do this
it's manual and ad hoc

00:20:28.983 --> 00:20:32.532
and therefore it's prone
to our own human bias and error.

00:20:32.532 --> 00:20:35.501
With Spinnaker, you can now
automatically promote

00:20:35.501 --> 00:20:40.045
or fail the canary,
minimizing your deployment risk

00:20:40.045 --> 00:20:43.837
while continuing to release
as fast as you possibly can.

00:20:43.837 --> 00:20:46.225
So, assuming that these changes look good,

00:20:46.225 --> 00:20:49.707
Spinnaker will automatically push
or roll back the canary.

00:20:50.130 --> 00:20:52.751
Using Spinnaker,
Waze was able to create

00:20:52.751 --> 00:20:56.228
over 1,000 production delivery pipelines

00:20:56.228 --> 00:20:59.880
while reducing their deployment errors
by almost 95%.

00:20:59.880 --> 00:21:01.463
That's pretty amazing.

00:21:02.098 --> 00:21:04.171
So the deployment...

00:21:04.171 --> 00:21:06.328
- (Morgan) ...is almost done.
- Almost done,

00:21:06.328 --> 00:21:08.407
but we want to show you one last thing,

00:21:08.407 --> 00:21:10.742
which is the Stackdriver Profiler has

00:21:10.742 --> 00:21:13.086
this really great comparison feature

00:21:13.086 --> 00:21:16.269
which lets you look
at CPU consumption patterns

00:21:16.269 --> 00:21:18.382
that change with inversions.

00:21:18.382 --> 00:21:20.859
And so you can see, we're running
this on some older data,

00:21:20.859 --> 00:21:23.705
but you can see how
slow functions will shrink.

00:21:23.705 --> 00:21:26.089
These are highlighted in blue.

00:21:26.577 --> 00:21:30.259
So let's go back to the Spinnaker Pipeline.

00:21:30.259 --> 00:21:31.701
(Morgan) Just a couple seconds left.

00:21:31.701 --> 00:21:33.486
(Melody) Couple seconds left.

00:21:33.486 --> 00:21:35.368
(Morgan) So I can show you
on this Profiler view

00:21:35.368 --> 00:21:37.404
the <i>parseCatalog</i> function that was taking up

00:21:37.404 --> 00:21:39.102
so much CPU time before,

00:21:39.102 --> 00:21:42.419
it's now effectively fallen to zero,
which is probably where it should be.

00:21:42.419 --> 00:21:44.301
So this comparison view lets us validate

00:21:44.301 --> 00:21:46.165
that on that second build that we built,

00:21:46.165 --> 00:21:48.947
even though it's from older data,
that we fixed that problem.

00:21:49.785 --> 00:21:52.121
So let's go take a look at Spinnaker again.

00:21:52.121 --> 00:21:54.283
Ah, it's still going.
A couple more seconds.

00:21:54.748 --> 00:21:56.754
(Melody) A couple more seconds.

00:21:57.719 --> 00:22:00.031
(Morgan) Now this is rolling out
on Spinnaker to, I think,

00:22:00.031 --> 00:22:02.196
five or six different instances
on Kubernetes.

00:22:02.196 --> 00:22:05.861
So, like Melody mentioned,
we took a canary build,

00:22:05.861 --> 00:22:08.219
so we took over one of those instances
with the test build,

00:22:08.219 --> 00:22:09.752
ran some checks on it.

00:22:09.752 --> 00:22:12.421
For the purpose of this demo
