00:32:25.325 --> 00:32:29.745
Marketing, I've done my job.
Alright, so... (laughs)

00:32:29.745 --> 00:32:31.431
So here's a Kubernetes cluster,

00:32:31.431 --> 00:32:32.991
but there's something interesting here.

00:32:32.991 --> 00:32:34.876
So there's a couple of tricks
that I like to do.

00:32:34.876 --> 00:32:36.352
Here's my Kubernetes cluster,

00:32:36.352 --> 00:32:38.387
and one thing you can do
that's really nice in GCP,

00:32:38.387 --> 00:32:40.511
you can actually have multiple node pools.

00:32:40.511 --> 00:32:43.379
So this is the 1.10.5 cluster.

00:32:43.379 --> 00:32:45.907
I have 13 nodes here,
but I actually have those nodes split

00:32:45.907 --> 00:32:48.483
between two different node pools.

00:32:48.483 --> 00:32:49.808
Now the reason why I'm doing this

00:32:49.808 --> 00:32:52.880
is I'm using their first general pool
for my web apps,

00:32:52.880 --> 00:32:55.887
my batch jobs, long-running services,

00:32:55.887 --> 00:32:58.668
but this other node pool...
... I'm a big fan of Envoy.

00:32:58.668 --> 00:33:00.594
So you've been hearing a lot about Istio,

00:33:00.594 --> 00:33:04.353
but underneath Istio there's this nice
reverse proxy called Envoy.

00:33:04.353 --> 00:33:05.852
And Envoy, if you can think about it,

00:33:05.852 --> 00:33:09.048
it's like a cloud-native replacement
for Nginx or HAProxy.

00:33:09.048 --> 00:33:11.747
It has all the things that I need
to do things like rate limiting

00:33:11.747 --> 00:33:13.313
and timeouts and retries.

00:33:13.313 --> 00:33:16.886
But I like to actually run Envoy
as my edge load balancer,

00:33:16.886 --> 00:33:18.417
even in GCP.

00:33:18.417 --> 00:33:20.282
It's the same load balancer
I run on my laptop.

00:33:20.282 --> 00:33:21.344
So I want some parity.

00:33:21.344 --> 00:33:24.693
So what I'm doing is I'm actually running
each of my Envoy instances

00:33:24.693 --> 00:33:26.657
across these three nodes

00:33:26.657 --> 00:33:28.275
using what we call a DaemonSet.

00:33:28.275 --> 00:33:30.640
And then the last thing I do
to set up my infrastructure?

00:33:30.640 --> 00:33:34.784
I create a load balancer
that points to just those three machines.

00:33:34.784 --> 00:33:35.969
So if you think about it,

00:33:35.969 --> 00:33:39.372
I have a network appliance
running inside of my cluster.

00:33:39.372 --> 00:33:42.505
No extra hops, no iptables--
straight to Envoy

00:33:42.505 --> 00:33:44.314
then directly to my apps.

00:33:44.314 --> 00:33:45.970
So, with the infrastructure in place

00:33:45.970 --> 00:33:48.034
we can start to think
about deploying our code.

00:33:48.034 --> 00:33:50.077
So let's go back to the command line
really quickly.

00:33:50.077 --> 00:33:54.721
So the next thing I want to do is
I don't want to execute this from my machine.

00:33:54.721 --> 00:33:56.547
So here's a cron job.

00:33:56.547 --> 00:33:57.934
Every five minutes it's going

00:33:57.934 --> 00:34:00.354
to kick that code off for me automatically.

00:34:00.354 --> 00:34:02.476
So my goal is just to push this cron Job up.

00:34:02.476 --> 00:34:04.887
But remember, the price
of admission to Kubernetes

00:34:04.887 --> 00:34:06.709
is a container image.

00:34:06.709 --> 00:34:08.770
Here's my second trick that I like to do.

00:34:08.770 --> 00:34:12.161
I have this saying,
"Don't ship build environments,

00:34:12.161 --> 00:34:13.647
ship artifacts."

00:34:13.647 --> 00:34:15.724
So when I build my containers

00:34:15.724 --> 00:34:19.000
I actually just build my binaries locally,

00:34:19.000 --> 00:34:20.595
upload the artifact,

00:34:20.595 --> 00:34:22.969
and only have Docker package it in an image

00:34:22.969 --> 00:34:24.978
with the scratch base image.

00:34:24.978 --> 00:34:27.751
So my images are roughly about 7 MB.

00:34:27.751 --> 00:34:30.161
Then watch how fast the builds are
if I do it this way.

00:34:30.161 --> 00:34:31.522
So the first thing I'm going to do

00:34:31.522 --> 00:34:33.061
is build that application locally,

00:34:33.061 --> 00:34:34.396
take that binary,

00:34:34.396 --> 00:34:37.454
push it up to Google Cloud Build,

00:34:37.454 --> 00:34:39.705
and Cloud Build is going to do
all the work for me.

00:34:39.705 --> 00:34:43.478
You'll notice another thing here--
I don't have Docker installed on my machine.

00:34:43.478 --> 00:34:47.027
I can just push my builds there,
and get all of my images.

00:34:47.027 --> 00:34:49.321
So in just a few seconds
we'll have a container image,

00:34:49.321 --> 00:34:50.505
we're ready to go,

00:34:50.505 --> 00:34:53.624
and then we can actually use that cron job
and Kubernetes will keep it running.

00:34:53.630 --> 00:34:57.670
So <i>kubectl apply -f cronjob.yaml</i>

00:34:58.377 --> 00:35:02.260
You'll notice there will be no pods created
until we hit that five-minute mark,

00:35:02.260 --> 00:35:05.173
but we'll start watching
just to see when things come up.

00:35:05.173 --> 00:35:08.335
So in about five minutes
you'll see the cron job kick off,

00:35:08.335 --> 00:35:12.251
and Kubernetes will keep a few of them around
just in case I need to troubleshoot.

00:35:12.251 --> 00:35:14.086
Alright, so the next thing
I'm going to do is--

00:35:14.086 --> 00:35:15.890
we're going to move a little bit faster now--

00:35:15.890 --> 00:35:18.465
is I need to put an API in front of this.

00:35:18.465 --> 00:35:21.512
So what I'm going to do here is say,
<i>kubectl apply -f</i>

00:35:21.512 --> 00:35:23.469
and I have this weather API service.

00:35:23.469 --> 00:35:26.365
And then what I'm going to do here
is just deploy that.

00:35:26.365 --> 00:35:29.708
And that's going to be a little bit faster
because I want it <i>right now</i>.

00:35:29.708 --> 00:35:31.662
I already have the image
on the container registry,

00:35:31.662 --> 00:35:34.929
it's going to pull that into the cluster,
and it's going to start running.

00:35:34.929 --> 00:35:37.666
Now here's the next cool thing
that you can do in GKE.

00:35:38.273 --> 00:35:41.745
We've done a lot of work
to make pods first-class citizens

00:35:41.745 --> 00:35:43.696
inside of Google Cloud.

00:35:43.696 --> 00:35:47.237
So you'll notice here every pod in Kubernetes
gets its own IP address.

00:35:47.237 --> 00:35:50.356
Now the one thing that we do
is we make those IPs native,

00:35:50.356 --> 00:35:52.897
using a technology we call IP aliases.

00:35:52.897 --> 00:35:55.969
And what IP aliases does
is it takes all those container IPs

00:35:55.969 --> 00:35:58.632
and makes them first-class in my VPC.

00:35:58.632 --> 00:36:02.474
So my development box
is a VM inside of my VPC.

00:36:02.474 --> 00:36:04.590
So what I should be able to do is actually,

00:36:04.590 --> 00:36:07.967
without any port forwarding or extra hops,

00:36:07.967 --> 00:36:10.435
I should be able
to make a call to my API servers

00:36:10.435 --> 00:36:12.225
using the container IP.

00:36:12.225 --> 00:36:13.249
And look at that.

00:36:13.249 --> 00:36:15.020
Directly talking to the container,

00:36:15.020 --> 00:36:17.025
using a very natural development flow

00:36:17.025 --> 00:36:18.528
where I can work on another app

00:36:18.528 --> 00:36:20.699
while talking to apps inside of a cluster.

00:36:20.699 --> 00:36:23.358
That's pretty dope, if you didn't think so.

00:36:23.358 --> 00:36:27.516
(cheers and applause)

00:36:27.516 --> 00:36:29.299
Alright, so we're moving along here,

00:36:29.299 --> 00:36:31.498
and the next thing we want to do
is get a web front end.

00:36:31.498 --> 00:36:34.182
Again, you kind of have established
some patterns here.

00:36:34.182 --> 00:36:36.402
Build a container, push it up,

00:36:36.402 --> 00:36:38.251
and describe to Kubernetes
what you want to do.

00:36:38.251 --> 00:36:40.224
So here we'll build our <i>weather - frontend</i>

00:36:40.224 --> 00:36:41.593
and then we'll do this <i>deployment</i>

00:36:41.593 --> 00:36:43.756
Now here's when things
get a little bit of interesting.

00:36:43.756 --> 00:36:45.041
We're not going to run one copy,

00:36:45.041 --> 00:36:46.843
we're going to let Kubernetes
run three copies

00:36:46.843 --> 00:36:48.296
across that cluster for us.

00:36:48.296 --> 00:36:50.000
Now once all of this running,

00:36:50.000 --> 00:36:53.847
I have Envoy in a pre-configured IP
and a DNS address

00:36:53.847 --> 00:36:56.260
that's pointing to this particular back end.

00:36:56.260 --> 00:36:59.116
So what I'm going to do now is pull this up,

00:36:59.116 --> 00:37:01.300
and we should be able
to hit the weather back end.

00:37:01.300 --> 00:37:03.424
Oh! Things are looking good.

00:37:04.234 --> 00:37:05.927
Let's see. Alright.

00:37:05.927 --> 00:37:08.095
Everything seems to be working.

00:37:08.095 --> 00:37:09.517
Now here's the thing.

00:37:09.517 --> 00:37:11.786
I've been the sole developer this whole time.

00:37:11.786 --> 00:37:14.701
I know the front end talks to the API server,

00:37:14.701 --> 00:37:17.187
and the API server talks to the database,

00:37:17.187 --> 00:37:19.106
but what happens if someone else on your team

00:37:19.106 --> 00:37:20.947
needs to check things out?

00:37:20.947 --> 00:37:23.312
The nice thing is I've been using OpenCensus.

00:37:23.312 --> 00:37:25.218
So, again, I don't actually have
a service mesh,

00:37:25.218 --> 00:37:28.076
but you can adopt
all these technologies à la carte.

00:37:28.076 --> 00:37:30.059
So if you want to add tracing
to your application,

00:37:30.059 --> 00:37:32.998
you can bring in tools
like the OpenCensus library,

00:37:32.998 --> 00:37:35.128
that allows you to decorate
your function calls

00:37:35.128 --> 00:37:36.764
or calls between apps,

00:37:36.764 --> 00:37:40.473
and send that data to tools
like Zipkin or Jaeger.

00:37:40.473 --> 00:37:44.478
In my case, I'm using Google Cloud,
so I'm going to send it to Stackdriver Trace.

00:37:44.478 --> 00:37:48.191
I'm going to pop over here
to the tracing console,

00:37:48.191 --> 00:37:50.908
and there we're going to find
one of those recent traces.

00:37:50.908 --> 00:37:53.641
So we can see one that just came in
roughly around now,

00:37:53.641 --> 00:37:54.763
and we'll click on it.

00:37:54.763 --> 00:37:56.893
And you can actually see the end-to-end.

00:37:56.893 --> 00:37:59.643
Traffic comes into the front end, the API,

00:37:59.643 --> 00:38:02.461
and then the API returns,
but not before talking to the database.

00:38:02.461 --> 00:38:03.880
Now here's the nice thing.

00:38:03.880 --> 00:38:07.947
If you trace this inside of your codebase
and I click <i>Show events</i>,

00:38:07.947 --> 00:38:10.764
I can come down here
and see the <i>exact</i> SQL query

00:38:10.764 --> 00:38:13.252
that's being executed at that particular hop.

00:38:13.252 --> 00:38:15.947
Now that gives you the ability
to actually see what's going on

00:38:15.947 --> 00:38:19.219
and give people a lot more insight,
without checking out the code.

00:38:19.999 --> 00:38:23.494
Alright, so we got
all the patterns in place--

00:38:23.494 --> 00:38:27.376
infrastructure, tracing--
and now we need to think about something new.

00:38:27.376 --> 00:38:29.346
Now, we only have about five minutes left

00:38:29.346 --> 00:38:31.079
so we're going to have to simulate this.

00:38:31.079 --> 00:38:34.405
When your boss shows up and it's like,
"Hey, we need some new functionality.

00:38:34.405 --> 00:38:37.269
Can you get it done by Friday?"

00:38:37.269 --> 00:38:38.804
So the task is on.

00:38:38.804 --> 00:38:41.835
So we have this infrastructure.
We got this nice pattern set up.

00:38:41.835 --> 00:38:42.815
And what we want to do now

00:38:42.815 --> 00:38:45.003
is start to think about
something cool we can add in.

00:38:45.003 --> 00:38:46.077
We've got a web front end,

00:38:46.077 --> 00:38:48.885
but that's like <i>so</i> 2017.

00:38:48.885 --> 00:38:51.151
You've got to have
a voice integration with your app

00:38:51.151 --> 00:38:52.849
if you want to be cool.

00:38:52.849 --> 00:38:54.609
If you can't talk to your application

00:38:54.609 --> 00:38:56.883
you're probably doing it wrong, okay?

00:38:56.883 --> 00:39:00.534
So what we want to do is think about
a tool called Dialogflow.

00:39:00.534 --> 00:39:02.138
You're not going to build

00:39:02.138 --> 00:39:06.245
your own natural-language
processing system from scratch.

00:39:06.245 --> 00:39:09.524
Some of you are probably trying. (chuckles)
There's a better way.

00:39:09.524 --> 00:39:11.024
So we're going to use Dialogflow.

00:39:11.024 --> 00:39:13.262
So, again, let's walk
through the pattern really quickly.

00:39:13.262 --> 00:39:15.920
We're going to write the application,

00:39:15.920 --> 00:39:18.693
and then we're going to build it
and push it up.

00:39:18.693 --> 00:39:21.900
Now remember, the container image
is just the price of admission.

00:39:21.900 --> 00:39:24.043
So we'll build this up.
Now here's the other thing.

00:39:24.043 --> 00:39:26.488
You won't be the sole developer

00:39:26.488 --> 00:39:28.989
working on this application forever.

00:39:28.989 --> 00:39:31.733
Maybe there's someone else on the team
that wants to contribute.

00:39:31.733 --> 00:39:35.419
And maybe they have no interest
in writing YAML files

00:39:35.419 --> 00:39:37.756
or using Kubernetes from the command line.

00:39:37.756 --> 00:39:42.399
We've done a lot of work to make sure
that Kubernetes works well from the UI.

00:39:42.399 --> 00:39:44.062
So we'll come over here.

00:39:44.062 --> 00:39:45.799
We'll go to the Kubernetes console.

00:39:45.799 --> 00:39:47.480
And we can click on <i>Workloads</i>.

00:39:47.480 --> 00:39:49.980
Now notice here all the things
I did on the command line

00:39:49.980 --> 00:39:53.264
are reflected in the GKE console.

00:39:53.264 --> 00:39:57.342
Now, if I have a new person on the team
that's not quite familiar with Kubernetes,

00:39:57.342 --> 00:39:58.511
this could be their workflow.

00:39:58.511 --> 00:40:00.233
We'll click <i>DEPLOY</i>.

00:40:00.233 --> 00:40:01.756
And we've done something special here.

00:40:01.756 --> 00:40:04.419
We've integrated deeply with GCP,

00:40:04.419 --> 00:40:06.694
including the Google Container Registry.

00:40:06.694 --> 00:40:07.885
So if I click on this,

00:40:07.885 --> 00:40:10.920
I can drop down and find
that image that I just created.

00:40:11.540 --> 00:40:13.552
It finds all the right tags.

00:40:13.552 --> 00:40:14.837
Hit <i>SELECT</i>.

00:40:14.837 --> 00:40:17.536
And then it fills out
everything I need underneath.

00:40:17.536 --> 00:40:19.278
Let's give this a name.

00:40:19.278 --> 00:40:21.365
We'll call it the <i>weather-assistant</i>.

00:40:22.055 --> 00:40:23.464
We have the right labels.

00:40:23.464 --> 00:40:26.682
And if I have multiple clusters
I can choose where it should deploy.

00:40:26.682 --> 00:40:28.947
If you want to see what's going on
underneath the hood,

00:40:28.947 --> 00:40:31.346
you can actually display
that sweet, sweet <i>YAML</i>

00:40:31.346 --> 00:40:33.381
that you love so much. (chuckles)

00:40:33.381 --> 00:40:35.059
And then we also give you an autoscaler

00:40:35.059 --> 00:40:37.779
to make sure you can respond
to traffic changes.

00:40:37.779 --> 00:40:39.553
And we'll hit <i>Deploy</i>.

00:40:39.553 --> 00:40:41.691
So that's going to do
all the heavy lifting for me

00:40:41.691 --> 00:40:43.173
right through the UI,

00:40:43.173 --> 00:40:45.341
and, just like that, it should be deployed.

00:40:45.341 --> 00:40:46.622
We'll switch over here.

00:40:46.622 --> 00:40:50.456
And we can see the <i>weather-assistant</i>
is now ready, serving traffic.

00:40:50.456 --> 00:40:53.054
Now the next thing we're going to do
is make sure that we're ready

00:40:53.054 --> 00:40:54.755
by checking the health check.

00:40:54.755 --> 00:40:58.891
So here's the DNS name
that I've assigned to this particular app.

00:40:58.891 --> 00:41:01.934
We'll hit it.
We see everything is working.

00:41:01.934 --> 00:41:04.784
And we can also see all my traffic
is flowing through Envoy.

00:41:04.784 --> 00:41:06.074
I should be in good shape.

00:41:06.074 --> 00:41:09.757
We only have a few minutes left.
Let's see if we can pull this last piece off.

00:41:09.757 --> 00:41:11.979
So I'm going to log in
to the Dialogflow console.

00:41:11.979 --> 00:41:14.218
This is also part
of the Google Cloud platform.

00:41:14.218 --> 00:41:17.919
So Dialogflow allows you to design
these voice integrations

00:41:17.919 --> 00:41:19.265
with your application.

00:41:19.265 --> 00:41:22.108
So we'll come here.
There's a couple things we need to do.

00:41:22.123 --> 00:41:23.459
First, we need to make sure

00:41:23.459 --> 00:41:25.928
that our users have a good time
with our application,

00:41:25.928 --> 00:41:26.973
so what we're going to do

00:41:26.973 --> 00:41:29.569
is we're going to define
all these verified inputs

00:41:29.569 --> 00:41:30.936
and create a few synonyms,

00:41:30.936 --> 00:41:33.181
just in case they want
to say things like, "Los Angeles,"

00:41:33.181 --> 00:41:37.275
or "Los Angeles, California, USA"
to talk to the weather app.

00:41:37.275 --> 00:41:40.205
The next thing we're going to do
is describe their intent.

00:41:40.205 --> 00:41:42.146
What does our user want to do?

00:41:42.146 --> 00:41:44.607
So here we want to have them
check the temperature.

00:41:44.607 --> 00:41:46.305
Now there's no way I'm going to define

00:41:46.305 --> 00:41:49.508
everything or every phrase
our users would do.

00:41:49.508 --> 00:41:50.633
So here what I'm going to do

00:41:50.633 --> 00:41:53.406
is give Dialogflow
a few training phrases.

00:41:53.406 --> 00:41:56.411
And notice how it's automatically
pulling out those regions

00:41:56.411 --> 00:41:59.124
that match the entities
that I described before.

00:41:59.124 --> 00:42:00.507
So we're ready to go.

00:42:00.507 --> 00:42:03.778
Now I want to send this to our webhook
that we just got deployed.

00:42:03.778 --> 00:42:06.091
So we'll come here.
We have a couple more steps.

00:42:06.091 --> 00:42:07.371
We'll go to <i>Fulfillment</i>.

00:42:07.371 --> 00:42:10.692
And from <i>Fulfillment</i> you'll see
that I'm just putting in

00:42:10.692 --> 00:42:12.695
that same URL that I had earlier

00:42:12.695 --> 00:42:15.039
that's pointing to my Kubernetes stack.

00:42:15.039 --> 00:42:16.457
Alright?

00:42:16.457 --> 00:42:18.479
You have all kinds of integrations
that you can do,

00:42:18.479 --> 00:42:21.119
but this time we just want to do
the Google Assistant.

00:42:21.119 --> 00:42:26.214
Now this whole time I've been using
my Pixelbook to do this demo,

00:42:26.214 --> 00:42:28.558
and this is something
that's also, I think, a part

00:42:28.558 --> 00:42:30.299
of the end-to-end experience at Google.

00:42:30.299 --> 00:42:31.901
So we want to test out this.

00:42:31.901 --> 00:42:35.311
Now, I could use a simulator,
or I could actually come down here

00:42:35.311 --> 00:42:37.116
and use my laptop.

00:42:37.116 --> 00:42:39.094
Now, to prove that this is real,

00:42:39.094 --> 00:42:41.020
we're going to look at the logs
for the Assistant

00:42:41.020 --> 00:42:43.049
to make sure Kelsey isn't cheating.

00:42:43.049 --> 00:42:47.764
So <i>kubectl logs</i>
we'll give it that container name,

00:42:47.764 --> 00:42:49.283
and we can just stream the logs here.

00:42:49.283 --> 00:42:51.667
So we can see it's listening
but there's nothing there yet.

00:42:51.667 --> 00:42:53.987
So if I hit the <i>Assistant</i> button
on my Pixelbook

00:42:54.987 --> 00:42:57.215
we should be able to talk to this thing.

00:42:58.496 --> 00:43:00.004
Yeah, whatever.

00:43:00.784 --> 00:43:04.056
Alright. "Talk to the local weather service."

00:43:04.733 --> 00:43:07.413
<i>(Google Assistant) Sure, here's
the test version of local weather.</i>

00:43:07.413 --> 00:43:08.351
(chime)

00:43:08.351 --> 00:43:09.739
<i>Hello, Kelsey.</i>

00:43:09.739 --> 00:43:11.224
(Kelsey) Hello!

00:43:13.335 --> 00:43:16.123
<i>(Google Assistant) Looks like you have
a pretty large crowd here.</i>

00:43:16.123 --> 00:43:18.392
<i>I hope the demo gods are on your side.</i>

00:43:18.577 --> 00:43:20.098
(cheers and applause)

00:43:20.338 --> 00:43:21.996
(Kelsey) I hope so, too!

00:43:22.569 --> 00:43:24.523
Alright, so let's give this a go.

00:43:24.947 --> 00:43:28.004
What's the current temperature
in Los Angeles, California?

00:43:28.496 --> 00:43:30.177
<i>(Google Assistant) The current temperature</i>

00:43:30.177 --> 00:43:35.021
<i>in Los Angeles, California, USA is 82℉</i>

00:43:35.021 --> 00:43:36.600
(Kelsey) Thank you.

00:43:37.262 --> 00:43:41.104
<i>(Google Assistant) Okay, I gotta admit.
That was pretty dope.</i>

00:43:41.104 --> 00:43:43.650
(laughter and applause)

00:43:44.930 --> 00:43:48.021
And with that you kind of saw
the end-to-end Google experience.

00:43:48.021 --> 00:43:50.987
Starting with my laptop
all the way to the data model,

00:43:50.987 --> 00:43:54.122
I can use open source tools
like Cloud SQL, Postgres.

00:43:54.122 --> 00:43:57.092
Then I can actually use
the industry-standard Kubernetes,

00:43:57.092 --> 00:43:59.020
backed by Kubernetes, or GKE.

00:43:59.020 --> 00:44:03.308
Then I have my choice of load balancers.
I can use familiar tools like Envoy or Nginx.

00:44:03.308 --> 00:44:05.294
And then I can use things like OpenCensus

00:44:05.294 --> 00:44:07.793
to add some visibility to my application

00:44:07.793 --> 00:44:10.726
and visualize all that in Stackdriver Trace.

00:44:10.726 --> 00:44:12.784
And then I can move on to try new ideas,

00:44:12.784 --> 00:44:15.146
like adding voice integration
to my application,

00:44:15.146 --> 00:44:17.518
all within the Google Cloud Platform.

00:44:17.518 --> 00:44:20.483
And with that, I'd like to introduce
our next speaker, Aja,

00:44:20.483 --> 00:44:22.741
who's going to come out
and talk more about languages.

00:44:22.741 --> 00:44:23.813
Thank you.

00:44:23.813 --> 00:44:25.834
(cheers and applause)

00:44:26.184 --> 00:44:29.900
♪ (music) ♪

00:44:40.087 --> 00:44:42.046
Hi, I'm Aja,

00:44:42.046 --> 00:44:44.171
and I lead our programming languages efforts

00:44:44.171 --> 00:44:48.406
on the Google Cloud Developer Advocacy team.

00:44:48.406 --> 00:44:51.041
I like working
in developer relations at Google

00:44:51.041 --> 00:44:54.347
because this job lets engineers, like myself,

00:44:54.347 --> 00:44:56.846
spend our time helping people
build cool things

00:44:56.846 --> 00:44:59.259
and giving back to our tech communities.

00:44:59.259 --> 00:45:01.456
My community has given me
a lot over the years,

00:45:01.456 --> 00:45:04.562
and I'm really grateful
that I get this chance to give back.

00:45:04.562 --> 00:45:08.229
And I work on languages because
I'm kind of a giant language nerd.

00:45:09.062 --> 00:45:11.809
I'd love to talk to you about
<i>all</i> the languages I enjoy using,

00:45:11.809 --> 00:45:14.995
but they only gave me time to talk to you
about two of them today.

00:45:14.995 --> 00:45:18.023
So the first language
we're going to talk about is Go.

00:45:18.724 --> 00:45:21.963
Go is growing <i>so</i> much right now.

00:45:21.963 --> 00:45:25.439
There are an estimated
one million gophers worldwide.

00:45:26.226 --> 00:45:29.229
But, more practically,
when I go to my local DevOps meetup,

00:45:29.229 --> 00:45:31.671
Go is the language we talk about the most.

00:45:31.671 --> 00:45:33.399
Folks are using it for lots of stuff,

00:45:33.399 --> 00:45:36.322
like building microservices
and improving their operations.

00:45:36.938 --> 00:45:39.462
And so I'm like,
"Hey, guys, what's with the Go?"

00:45:39.462 --> 00:45:41.681
And they give me lots
of reasons they like it.

00:45:42.051 --> 00:45:45.407
First off, it's the language
of the containers world.

00:45:45.681 --> 00:45:48.827
Almost everything Kelsey just showed
was written in Go,

00:45:48.827 --> 00:45:50.779
including Docker and Kubernetes.

00:45:50.779 --> 00:45:53.055
And Go is great for systems-level programming

00:45:53.055 --> 00:45:55.589
because it's fast, and it was built
from the ground up

00:45:55.589 --> 00:45:57.102
with concurrency support.

00:45:57.102 --> 00:45:58.402
For those coming from languages

00:45:58.402 --> 00:46:00.659
where concurrency
was kind of tacked on later,

00:46:00.659 --> 00:46:04.446
having concurrency as a first-class concept
is really delightful.

00:46:05.147 --> 00:46:08.085
And folks also like that Go
is developer-friendly.

00:46:08.085 --> 00:46:11.666
There are very few keywords,
and it uses familiar syntax.

00:46:12.326 --> 00:46:14.620
And gophers rave about
how easy it is to write,

00:46:14.620 --> 00:46:18.876
how it has the speed of a compiled language
and all the benefits of static typing.

00:46:18.886 --> 00:46:23.034
And all of that leads to faster performance,
reduced QA time,

00:46:23.034 --> 00:46:25.222
and fewer errors in production.

00:46:25.222 --> 00:46:28.102
Personally, my favorite thing about Go

00:46:28.102 --> 00:46:30.119
is the welcoming and supportive community

00:46:30.119 --> 00:46:32.594
of passionate gophers throughout the world.

00:46:33.778 --> 00:46:38.244
So, today I want to talk about the new
Go Cloud Project from Google.

00:46:38.914 --> 00:46:42.615
This library provides gophers
with cloud-agnostic abstractions

00:46:42.615 --> 00:46:46.532
for services like relational data stores,
runtime configs and blob storage.

00:46:46.978 --> 00:46:48.878
So that's a bunch of words,

00:46:48.878 --> 00:46:50.669
but let's say you have an application

00:46:50.669 --> 00:46:52.191
that uses Google Cloud Storage

00:46:52.191 --> 00:46:54.791
and storage on some other provider.

00:46:54.791 --> 00:46:56.867
Currently you have to have
two branches in your code,

00:46:56.867 --> 00:46:58.227
one for each provider.

00:46:58.227 --> 00:46:59.992
You have to remember that maybe one provider

00:46:59.992 --> 00:47:03.065
uses the method <i>ListBucketContents</i>
to see what's in a bucket

00:47:03.065 --> 00:47:06.038
and the other uses something, 
I don't know, maybe <i>describe-bucket</i>

00:47:06.038 --> 00:47:07.623
and it's doing the same thing

00:47:07.623 --> 00:47:09.773
but I don't want to keep all that in my head.

00:47:09.773 --> 00:47:10.991
I'm lazy.

00:47:11.619 --> 00:47:15.757
And the Go Cloud Project helps with this
by providing one set of objects and methods

00:47:15.757 --> 00:47:18.161
that work with multiple cloud providers.

00:47:18.161 --> 00:47:21.264
You don't have to learn an API
for each provider.

00:47:21.264 --> 00:47:22.502
You can use one method

00:47:22.502 --> 00:47:24.675
and the Go Cloud Project
takes care of translating it

00:47:24.675 --> 00:47:26.903
to the right method for that cloud,

00:47:26.903 --> 00:47:29.516
or maybe even multiple methods.

00:47:29.516 --> 00:47:32.100
And the return data
is consistent between clouds,

00:47:32.100 --> 00:47:35.999
so you don't have to worry about doing
all sorts of cloud-specific type checking.

00:47:36.860 --> 00:47:39.340
The big thing, though,
is that with the Go Cloud Project

00:47:39.340 --> 00:47:41.489
your application code
doesn't need to be aware

00:47:41.489 --> 00:47:42.872
of what back end you're using,

00:47:42.872 --> 00:47:45.087
or even if you're using multiple clouds.

00:47:45.743 --> 00:47:48.649
The API it provides allows you
to set up your application code

00:47:48.649 --> 00:47:52.462
from the configuration needed
to connect to the cloud

00:47:52.462 --> 00:47:55.730
so you can focus on the fun stuff,
like adding new features.

00:47:55.730 --> 00:47:58.213
Ino, from the Advocacy team,
is with us today,

00:47:58.213 --> 00:48:00.791
and he's going to demonstrate
the Go Cloud Project.

00:48:00.791 --> 00:48:05.680
(cheers and applause)

00:48:06.497 --> 00:48:11.093
So, Ino has an application
that uploads a photo to cloud storage

00:48:11.093 --> 00:48:13.719
on either GCP or AWS.

00:48:18.258 --> 00:48:21.242
And, of course, we're going
to upload a picture of a gopher.

00:48:23.586 --> 00:48:26.374
So, here's the application code
that runs that.

00:48:28.783 --> 00:48:30.889
And you can see that in each method

00:48:30.889 --> 00:48:32.652
we have a top-level conditional

00:48:32.652 --> 00:48:35.484
for choosing between
the two clouds we have to support.

00:48:35.879 --> 00:48:38.519
And under each branch the code is similar,

00:48:38.519 --> 00:48:40.208
but it's not entirely the same.

00:48:40.208 --> 00:48:42.361
Each cloud is initialized
a little differently

00:48:42.361 --> 00:48:44.118
and it provides objects
with different methods

00:48:44.118 --> 00:48:46.792
for handling the results
you get back from storage.

00:48:46.792 --> 00:48:48.591
And I've written code like this,

00:48:48.591 --> 00:48:52.279
but I'm not particularly proud of it,
because it's really gross.

00:48:52.279 --> 00:48:53.534
And here's the thing.

00:48:53.534 --> 00:48:55.403
We can do a lot better.

00:48:56.317 --> 00:48:59.523
So here's a version of the code
that uses the Go Cloud Project.

00:49:00.019 --> 00:49:04.244
The first thing you're going to see
is that that top-level conditional is gone.

00:49:04.252 --> 00:49:06.589
What we've done is we've separated
the application logic

00:49:06.589 --> 00:49:08.875
from the storage configuration.

00:49:08.875 --> 00:49:10.493
And to make it really clear,

00:49:11.404 --> 00:49:13.632
here's the <i>diff</i> between the two versions.

00:49:15.785 --> 00:49:18.963
Adam promised you all a <i>diff</i>.
Here's the <i>diff</i>.

00:49:19.590 --> 00:49:21.733
You can see that there's a lot less code

00:49:21.733 --> 00:49:24.090
in the version that uses
the Go Cloud library.

00:49:24.090 --> 00:49:27.000
And, even better,
the code that uses the library

00:49:27.000 --> 00:49:30.499
has a better design, because
we've separated the two concerns.

00:49:30.499 --> 00:49:33.133
Better engineering practices
lead to more maintainable code.

00:49:33.137 --> 00:49:34.454
Thank you, Ino!

00:49:35.442 --> 00:49:39.129
(applause)

00:49:41.112 --> 00:49:44.531
If you've ever had to support multiple clouds
in a single application,

00:49:44.531 --> 00:49:47.058
you know that using a library
like the Go Cloud Project

00:49:47.058 --> 00:49:51.168
is a much easier way to do things,
and it encourages good engineering practice.

00:49:51.178 --> 00:49:54.965
But library authors can also build
on top of the Go Cloud Project

00:49:54.965 --> 00:49:59.251
to ensure that folks on any provider
can use their library.

00:49:59.251 --> 00:50:01.522
Our hope is that this project will foster

00:50:01.522 --> 00:50:03.026
an ecosystem of libraries

00:50:03.026 --> 00:50:06.373
built with multi-cloud portability in mind.

00:50:06.373 --> 00:50:09.251
The Go Cloud Project is,
of course, open source,

00:50:09.251 --> 00:50:14.023
currently supports AWS and GCP,
but more clouds are coming soon.

00:50:14.023 --> 00:50:16.532
And, of course, contributions welcome.

00:50:17.338 --> 00:50:20.779
So, the second language I want
to talk about today is JavaScript.

00:50:20.779 --> 00:50:24.902
JavaScript was the most popular language
on GitHub last year.

00:50:24.902 --> 00:50:27.906
There were so many more JavaScript commits

00:50:27.906 --> 00:50:30.979
as there were for any other language--
it's really remarkable.

00:50:30.979 --> 00:50:33.915
And I know I've written a bunch
of JavaScript in my career

00:50:33.915 --> 00:50:36.171
and I'm assuming most of you have as well.

00:50:37.509 --> 00:50:39.648
Google has been a champion
of the JavaScript language

00:50:39.648 --> 00:50:41.506
for a really long time.

00:50:41.506 --> 00:50:45.670
Some of Google's JavaScript projects
include Angular, AMP, Polymer,

00:50:45.670 --> 00:50:48.776
and the V8 engine
that's at the heart of Node.js.

00:50:49.731 --> 00:50:52.154
And JavaScript is one
of those fantastic languages

00:50:52.154 --> 00:50:55.322
that you can use just about anywhere
for just about anything.

00:50:55.322 --> 00:50:57.200
If you'd told me in 1995

00:50:57.200 --> 00:50:59.006
that I would be writing
server-side JavaScript

00:50:59.006 --> 00:51:00.515
I wouldn't have believed you.

00:51:01.429 --> 00:51:03.642
But the async features
of something like Node.js

00:51:03.642 --> 00:51:08.318
help you build fast, scaleable
web application back ends,

00:51:08.318 --> 00:51:12.021
and Node.js is also the default language
for Functions as a Service.

00:51:12.021 --> 00:51:16.246
But it can be used for non-web
applications too, like robotics.

00:51:16.246 --> 00:51:19.456
And, of course, JavaScript
is as popular as ever

00:51:19.456 --> 00:51:23.243
for building rich client-side
interactive experiences.

00:51:23.243 --> 00:51:25.283
So, if you go out and learn JavaScript

00:51:25.283 --> 00:51:27.756
there really isn't much you can't do.

00:51:28.924 --> 00:51:31.850
The community has been asking
for Node.js on App Engine standard,

00:51:31.850 --> 00:51:34.031
and we're happy to say that it's in beta.

00:51:34.031 --> 00:51:36.717
Node 8 can be used just like
your other App Engine language

00:51:36.717 --> 00:51:39.308
and you can use most
of your favorite MPM modules.

00:51:39.768 --> 00:51:41.491
And if you don't know,

00:51:41.491 --> 00:51:44.321
App Engine is GCP's
Platform as a Service product.

00:51:44.321 --> 00:51:47.905
It's been around for a long time--
we just celebrated the 10th anniversary--

00:51:47.905 --> 00:51:51.359
and all of the fans of App Engine
like the ease of use,

00:51:51.359 --> 00:51:54.358
autoscaling, security,
and how well it integrates

00:51:54.358 --> 00:51:57.496
with all the other GCP products,
like Datastore.

00:51:57.496 --> 00:51:59.728
And with this, Node.js now runs

00:51:59.728 --> 00:52:02.979
on every single compute platform
the GCP offers.

00:52:02.979 --> 00:52:05.410
I told you you could do anything with Node!

00:52:06.247 --> 00:52:07.913
So, it's really important to me,

00:52:07.913 --> 00:52:10.153
and all of my friends over there
dressed in red

00:52:10.153 --> 00:52:11.736
on the Cloud Developer Relations team,

00:52:11.736 --> 00:52:14.339
(cheers and applause)

00:52:14.339 --> 00:52:17.470
that Google is an active participant
and a good citizen

00:52:17.470 --> 00:52:19.515
in our developer communities.

00:52:19.515 --> 00:52:22.274
After all, the best tools
are written by developers

00:52:22.274 --> 00:52:25.549
who understand a community's
needs and idiosyncrasies.

00:52:26.119 --> 00:52:28.799
And we like to put our code
where our mouth is.

00:52:28.799 --> 00:52:31.091
And we do that by contributing
to improving the languages

00:52:31.091 --> 00:52:33.202
and surrounding ecosystems.

00:52:33.202 --> 00:52:35.776
So that's why Google Cloud
is a Platinum Sponsor

00:52:35.776 --> 00:52:37.172
of the Node Foundation,

00:52:37.172 --> 00:52:40.201
and it's also why there are many googlers
participating in Node,

00:52:40.201 --> 00:52:43.497
in a variety of roles--
giving their time and expertise

00:52:43.497 --> 00:52:45.556
to help guide the future of the language.

00:52:45.912 --> 00:52:47.970
They only gave me
a couple of minutes today,

00:52:47.970 --> 00:52:50.841
so I was only able to talk about a few
of the popular languages.

00:52:50.841 --> 00:52:54.653
And even then, I only focused
on their server-side uses.

00:52:54.653 --> 00:52:58.403
But client and mobile development
are important and growing too.

00:52:58.403 --> 00:53:00.332
So next you're going to hear
from Sam and Francis,

00:53:00.332 --> 00:53:02.371
and they're going to talk
about Firebase development

00:53:02.371 --> 00:53:04.666
and development
with GCP products for mobile.

00:53:05.651 --> 00:53:09.103
♪ (music) ♪

00:53:18.432 --> 00:53:19.924
Thanks, Aja.

00:53:19.924 --> 00:53:23.729
Our mission for Firebase is
to help mobile app teams succeed

00:53:23.729 --> 00:53:26.774
by providing you a platform
to help solve the key problems

00:53:26.774 --> 00:53:30.805
across the lifecycle of your app--
from building your app,

00:53:30.805 --> 00:53:34.077
to improving your app quality,
to growing your business.

00:53:34.712 --> 00:53:37.072
We've come a long way since two years ago,

00:53:37.072 --> 00:53:39.943
when we expanded Firebase
from a real-time database

00:53:39.943 --> 00:53:42.389
to a broad app-development platform.

00:53:42.389 --> 00:53:46.778
And it's so exciting to see
there are now over 1.2 million apps

00:53:46.778 --> 00:53:49.911
actively using Firebase every month,

00:53:49.911 --> 00:53:52.900
including many of the top
Android and iOS apps

00:53:52.900 --> 00:53:56.367
like The New York TImes, Box, and Pandora.

00:53:56.367 --> 00:53:57.773
Now as you've heard earlier,

00:53:57.773 --> 00:54:00.436
Google Cloud offers
a wide variety of products

00:54:00.436 --> 00:54:04.211
to help you build secure
and scaleable infrastructure.

00:54:04.211 --> 00:54:06.991
So when it comes time to build
your mobile app for your business,

00:54:06.991 --> 00:54:09.739
Firebase integrates smoothly with GCP

00:54:09.739 --> 00:54:12.594
to help you build it better and faster.

00:54:12.594 --> 00:54:15.240
To show you how easy it is
to build an app with Firebase,

00:54:15.240 --> 00:54:19.285
I'd like to welcome
one of my teammates, Sam, to join us.

00:54:19.285 --> 00:54:21.902
(cheers and applause)

00:54:21.902 --> 00:54:24.334
(Sam) Hello. Thank you, Francis.

00:54:24.334 --> 00:54:28.090
So, one of the first problems
that most mobile app developers face

00:54:28.090 --> 00:54:31.097
is finding a way to store and sync
their data in the cloud,

00:54:31.097 --> 00:54:33.675
ideally without managing any infrastructure.

00:54:33.675 --> 00:54:35.543
Cloud Firestore was built
to solve this problem

00:54:35.543 --> 00:54:38.035
on every platform that you work on.

00:54:38.035 --> 00:54:39.838
It's a fully managed noSQL database

00:54:39.838 --> 00:54:42.543
built on Google's global infrastructure.

00:54:42.543 --> 00:54:45.360
Now, let's say I'm the developer
of a financial services app

00:54:45.360 --> 00:54:47.319
called NextWallet.

00:54:47.319 --> 00:54:50.336
My users have been asking me for a long time
to add a mobile app

00:54:50.336 --> 00:54:52.205
to complement the web app.

00:54:52.205 --> 00:54:55.607
Firebase helps you build apps
on Android, iOS, and the web,

00:54:55.607 --> 00:54:59.130
but today I'm just going to focus
on building an Android app.

00:54:59.130 --> 00:55:01.476
So here we are in the Firebase console.

00:55:01.476 --> 00:55:04.506
And you can see I've already got
all my data in Cloud Firestore.

00:55:04.506 --> 00:55:06.488
This is a list of my recent transactions.

00:55:06.488 --> 00:55:09.848
I went to Sam's Place, and I spent about $45.

00:55:10.813 --> 00:55:13.092
Moving over to Android Studio,

00:55:13.653 --> 00:55:15.777
I want to load all of my transactions here.

00:55:15.777 --> 00:55:18.100
I want to display
the user's recent transactions.

00:55:18.100 --> 00:55:19.600
So let's do that.

00:55:20.691 --> 00:55:21.932
Here...

00:55:26.337 --> 00:55:28.453
... Okay, so what I'm doing here
is I'm getting the ID

00:55:28.453 --> 00:55:30.129
of the currently signed-in user.

00:55:30.129 --> 00:55:32.308
And then you can see here I'm forming a query

00:55:32.308 --> 00:55:34.948
using the Firestore Android SDK.

00:55:34.948 --> 00:55:37.338
We're asking for the user's
latest 50 transactions,

00:55:37.338 --> 00:55:39.044
ordered by time.

00:55:39.044 --> 00:55:42.002
You'll also notice I can query this data
directly from my app,

00:55:42.002 --> 00:55:44.920
without building any API servers or proxies.

00:55:44.920 --> 00:55:48.909
I'm accessing the remote data
as if it was in a local database.

00:55:48.909 --> 00:55:52.182
So, if I run this in the emulator,

00:55:53.832 --> 00:55:56.075
you can see that all of the transactions
from the console

00:55:56.075 --> 00:55:57.827
are now displayed in the app.

00:55:57.827 --> 00:56:00.758
And this connection is completely
real-time by default.

00:56:00.758 --> 00:56:02.692
So if I go to the console

00:56:02.692 --> 00:56:04.998
and change the name of one of the places--

00:56:04.998 --> 00:56:08.230
change <i>Sam's Place</i> to <i>Sam's Awesome Place</i>,

00:56:08.230 --> 00:56:11.847
it updated in the app
before we could even switch screens.

00:56:13.492 --> 00:56:15.925
So with barely any code,

00:56:15.925 --> 00:56:18.799
my mobile app is showing
the same data as my web app,

00:56:18.799 --> 00:56:20.207
in real time.

00:56:20.830 --> 00:56:24.785
Now, if you're used to accessing
a database via a REST API,

00:56:24.785 --> 00:56:27.589
you may have found it strange
I did not include any tokens

00:56:27.589 --> 00:56:29.340
or headers in my request.

00:56:29.340 --> 00:56:31.774
That's because Cloud Firestore
automatically detects

00:56:31.774 --> 00:56:34.227
that I'm signed in
with Firebase authentication

00:56:34.227 --> 00:56:36.949
and includes that auth state transparently.

00:56:36.949 --> 00:56:39.290
So every request from my app
to Cloud Firestore

00:56:39.290 --> 00:56:42.681
is checked by Firebase rules,
which is a declarative system

00:56:42.681 --> 00:56:45.435
for controlling access to Firebase resources.

00:56:45.435 --> 00:56:48.108
So let's see the rules
that are keeping this app safe.

00:56:48.471 --> 00:56:50.497
So these are my custom security rules.

00:56:50.497 --> 00:56:54.210
Here, I'm making sure that users
can only read and write their own data.

00:56:54.210 --> 00:56:57.090
And here I've locked down rights
to transactional information

00:56:57.090 --> 00:56:59.065
to prevent any sort of spoofing.

00:56:59.065 --> 00:57:02.864
It's really powerful to have
direct client access to your data

00:57:02.864 --> 00:57:04.888
without running any authentication servers

00:57:04.888 --> 00:57:06.709
or API proxies.

00:57:06.709 --> 00:57:08.913
It completely streamlines
your development process.

00:57:08.913 --> 00:57:11.885
And, even more importantly,
it reduces the surface for bugs

00:57:11.885 --> 00:57:13.860
or for vulnerabilities.

00:57:14.591 --> 00:57:17.473
Now, with sensitive applications
like this finance app,

00:57:17.473 --> 00:57:20.092
you can't do everything on the client side.

00:57:20.092 --> 00:57:22.374
If I need to add some critical business logic

00:57:22.374 --> 00:57:24.374
I want to run it in a trusted location.

00:57:24.801 --> 00:57:28.450
So the newest feature in my app
is that users can pay their bills.

00:57:28.450 --> 00:57:30.819
And, unlike viewing recent transactions,

00:57:30.819 --> 00:57:33.459
this is a really privileged operation.

00:57:33.459 --> 00:57:37.549
So, moving here, I'll write
a serverless Node.js function

00:57:37.549 --> 00:57:39.823
to pay a bill from the user's bank account

00:57:39.823 --> 00:57:41.199
and then mark it as paid,

00:57:41.199 --> 00:57:43.521
and I'll deploy this whole thing
to Cloud Functions.

00:57:43.521 --> 00:57:46.963
You can see here that I've got a function.
It's called <i>payBill</i>.

00:57:46.963 --> 00:57:50.643
And after the bill is paid it marks
the <i>paid</i> state to <i>true</i>

00:57:50.643 --> 00:57:52.313
in Firestore.

00:57:52.313 --> 00:57:55.441
Now, in the Android app

00:57:56.757 --> 00:57:59.388
we can simply call this function
by its name, <i>payBill</i>,

00:57:59.390 --> 00:58:00.670
the same name I defined,

00:58:00.670 --> 00:58:03.985
and we can pass in the ID
of the bill we want to pay.

00:58:03.985 --> 00:58:05.691
After the function runs,

00:58:05.691 --> 00:58:08.737
I'll get this callback here with the result.

00:58:08.737 --> 00:58:11.226
Just like with the data call
to Cloud Firestore,

00:58:11.226 --> 00:58:14.315
our authentication state is passed
seamlessly in the request,

00:58:14.315 --> 00:58:17.226
so users can only access their own bills.

00:58:17.226 --> 00:58:20.050
So now let's use it.
Let's pay a bill.

00:58:20.739 --> 00:58:24.077
Hit this here, and what just happened
was the Cloud Function ran

00:58:24.077 --> 00:58:25.222
and made the update,

00:58:25.222 --> 00:58:28.081
which was detected
by the client in real time.

00:58:28.081 --> 00:58:30.039
So in just the last few minutes

00:58:30.039 --> 00:58:33.513
we set up a secure, scaleable
and real-time connection to our data,

00:58:33.513 --> 00:58:36.666
and also deployed a Cloud Function
to perform trusted actions,

00:58:36.666 --> 00:58:38.708
which is pretty impressive, right?

00:58:39.216 --> 00:58:42.188
(cheers and applause)

00:58:42.438 --> 00:58:44.582
(Francis) Once your app is built,

00:58:44.582 --> 00:58:48.398
you'll likely be looking to improve
your app's stability and performance,

00:58:48.398 --> 00:58:50.493
since the first impression the app makes

00:58:50.493 --> 00:58:53.080
is crucial to the long-term success
of your business.

00:58:53.080 --> 00:58:57.539
Now, we've seen through Google Play
that 42% of one-star reviews

00:58:57.539 --> 00:59:00.347
mentioned instability or bugs,

00:59:00.347 --> 00:59:04.844
whereas 73% of five-star reviews
mentioned design,

00:59:04.844 --> 00:59:07.066
usability, or speed.

00:59:07.066 --> 00:59:11.495
So having a stable and speedy app
is essential to your success.

00:59:11.495 --> 00:59:14.322
So let's switch it back over to Sam
to take a look at some of the ways

00:59:14.322 --> 00:59:17.192
that Firebase helps you
improve your app quality.

00:59:18.177 --> 00:59:22.159
So as a mobile developer
I have to release my code into the wild,

00:59:22.159 --> 00:59:25.422
and it could end up
on any kind of device or any network.

00:59:25.422 --> 00:59:27.101
I need to know how it's performing,

00:59:27.101 --> 00:59:29.500
and, more importantly,
I need to know when it's crashing.

00:59:29.500 --> 00:59:32.271
If I wait to hear about crashes
in bad Play Store reviews,

00:59:32.271 --> 00:59:33.507
that's way too late.

00:59:33.507 --> 00:59:36.274
So all I have to do
to track crashes in this app

00:59:36.274 --> 00:59:39.043
is add this single-line dependency
on Crashlytics.

00:59:39.043 --> 00:59:42.058
I don't have to write a line
of real Android code.

00:59:42.479 --> 00:59:44.375
Now, let's go into the Firebase console

00:59:44.375 --> 00:59:46.914
and see what crashes
are happening for my users.

00:59:47.400 --> 00:59:51.493
So here I can see that I had a crash,
but I don't know why yet.

00:59:51.493 --> 00:59:53.350
Let's take a look at the trace.

00:59:53.350 --> 00:59:57.319
I can see that it was a fatal exception.
I can see the exact line where it occurred.

00:59:57.319 --> 00:59:59.756
I can also see some information
about the device,

00:59:59.756 --> 01:00:01.685
where it happened, and the environment.

01:00:01.685 --> 01:00:03.262
Through an integration with Slack

01:00:03.262 --> 01:00:06.675
I can get new crashes like this
sent to me and my team in real time.

01:00:07.129 --> 01:00:09.448
Now, here in the <i>Logs</i> panel,

01:00:09.448 --> 01:00:12.080
I can see we have a list of analytics events

01:00:12.080 --> 01:00:13.878
that preceded the crash.

01:00:13.878 --> 01:00:18.247
Event logging from Google Analytics
to Crashlytics is automatic

01:00:18.247 --> 01:00:20.485
and it provides really helpful context.

01:00:20.573 --> 01:00:22.200
So now I have a ton of information

01:00:22.200 --> 01:00:24.484
on what the user is doing to cause the crash,

01:00:24.484 --> 01:00:27.581
which allows me to debug my app
more efficiently.

01:00:27.581 --> 01:00:29.792
I can spend more of my time
and my team's resources

01:00:29.792 --> 01:00:32.113
on building features for my users.

01:00:32.743 --> 01:00:35.681
And now that we can measure
if the app is stable,

01:00:35.681 --> 01:00:38.636
I still need to directly measure
the goals of my business.

01:00:38.636 --> 01:00:41.314
So for the new Bill Pay feature
I want to make sure

01:00:41.314 --> 01:00:43.724
that users are able
to find the tab in the app,

01:00:43.724 --> 01:00:47.046
pay the bill,
and that it all runs successfully.

01:00:47.046 --> 01:00:50.251
Firebase provides free
unlimited mobile analytics

01:00:50.251 --> 01:00:52.782
and automatically measures
common events in your app.

01:00:52.782 --> 01:00:55.524
But I want to add custom analytics
for each Bill Pay event

01:00:55.524 --> 01:00:57.744
for the attempt, success, and the failure

01:00:57.744 --> 01:00:59.944
so I can track user behavior here.

01:00:59.944 --> 01:01:02.184
So if we move back into Android Studio,

01:01:03.034 --> 01:01:05.323
in the Bill Pay flow I'll add calls

01:01:05.323 --> 01:01:07.037
around the common parts of the flow.

01:01:07.037 --> 01:01:09.501
So here I'll log the attempt.

01:01:10.620 --> 01:01:12.770
Here I'll log when it succeeds.

01:01:13.345 --> 01:01:15.358
And here I'll log when it fails.

01:01:16.462 --> 01:01:18.496
You can see that for each event
I just pick a name

01:01:18.496 --> 01:01:21.340
and then I pass a bundle of parameters.

01:01:21.340 --> 01:01:25.159
Now that the code's running,
if I go back into the app and pay a bill,

01:01:25.159 --> 01:01:26.993
those events will fire.

01:01:28.310 --> 01:01:29.830
Heading back into the console,

01:01:29.830 --> 01:01:32.694
I can see these events
coming in, in real time.

01:01:33.254 --> 01:01:34.534
By defining custom events,

01:01:34.534 --> 01:01:37.747
you can measure the things
that uniquely matter to your business.

01:01:37.747 --> 01:01:39.775
Analytics is the backbone of Firebase

01:01:39.775 --> 01:01:42.247
and the foundation for a successful business.

01:01:44.165 --> 01:01:45.883
(Francis) Having the right analytics

01:01:45.883 --> 01:01:49.371
is the first step to ensure
your users are happy

01:01:49.371 --> 01:01:51.909
and your app is doing the job
you want it to do.

01:01:51.909 --> 01:01:53.317
But once you have the data,

01:01:53.317 --> 01:01:55.592
you'll want to put it to work
for your business.

01:01:55.592 --> 01:01:59.929
With Firebase, you can export
your analytics data into BigQuery,

01:01:59.929 --> 01:02:03.811
join it with other data sources,
and perform deeper analysis.

01:02:04.216 --> 01:02:08.335
You can also take action right directly
from the Firebase console,

01:02:08.335 --> 01:02:10.915
and it comes with a built-in
A/B testing framework.

01:02:10.915 --> 01:02:13.416
So, for example,
you can send push notifications

01:02:13.416 --> 01:02:16.010
or test different configurations of your app

01:02:16.010 --> 01:02:19.601
without having to redeploy
to the app store or Google Play.

01:02:19.601 --> 01:02:22.561
With A/B testing,
you can define different variants,

01:02:22.561 --> 01:02:25.811
set the primary and secondary goals
for your experiments,

01:02:25.811 --> 01:02:28.846
and the system will automatically
do the hard work for you

01:02:28.846 --> 01:02:31.399
to help you find the optimal variant.

01:02:31.399 --> 01:02:32.810
And once you have the winner,

01:02:32.810 --> 01:02:35.752
you can roll that experience out
to the rest of your user base

01:02:35.752 --> 01:02:37.472
without another app deployment.

01:02:38.352 --> 01:02:40.651
Firebase also helps you take targeted action

01:02:40.651 --> 01:02:43.004
by leveraging machine learning.

01:02:43.004 --> 01:02:46.830
Now, it's one thing to take action
based on past user behavior,

01:02:46.830 --> 01:02:48.743
but with Firebase Predictions

01:02:48.743 --> 01:02:51.623
you can apply ML to your analytics data

01:02:51.623 --> 01:02:56.135
to take targeted actions
based on predicted <i>future</i> behavior.

01:02:56.135 --> 01:02:58.636
So, for example, you can send special offers

01:02:58.636 --> 01:03:01.637
to users who are likely to leave your app.

01:03:01.637 --> 01:03:04.523
Or, in Sam's example,
he can send push notifications

01:03:04.523 --> 01:03:08.085
targeted to users who are likely
to miss a bill payment.

01:03:08.772 --> 01:03:11.122
So Predictions was
the first step that we took

01:03:11.122 --> 01:03:14.316
to bring the power
of Google's ML to work for you.

01:03:14.316 --> 01:03:16.883
Two months ago we took our second step,

01:03:16.883 --> 01:03:20.229
with the release of ML Kit in public beta.

01:03:20.229 --> 01:03:23.688
With ML Kit we're bringing together
machine learning technologies

01:03:23.688 --> 01:03:25.192
from across Google,

01:03:25.192 --> 01:03:27.685
and making that available
to every mobile developer

01:03:27.685 --> 01:03:30.788
working on Android and iOS.

01:03:30.788 --> 01:03:33.935
ML Kit comes with five APIs out of the box,

01:03:33.935 --> 01:03:36.460
like image labeling and text recognition,

01:03:36.460 --> 01:03:40.336
and you can run these APIs on-device
for low-latency processing

01:03:40.336 --> 01:03:43.748
or in the cloud
for higher levels of accuracy.

01:03:44.017 --> 01:03:45.966
And if you have more sophisticated needs,

01:03:45.966 --> 01:03:48.960
you can upload your own
custom TensorFlow Lite model

01:03:48.960 --> 01:03:52.050
and serve that through Google's
global infrastructure.

01:03:52.050 --> 01:03:54.328
Your app can dynamically
retrieve these models

01:03:54.328 --> 01:03:56.088
and evaluate them on-device,

01:03:56.088 --> 01:03:59.749
so that means you don't need
to bundle the model with your app binary

01:03:59.749 --> 01:04:03.754
and you can update it
without pushing an app updates.

01:04:03.754 --> 01:04:06.843
And since ML Kit is available
through Firebase,

01:04:06.843 --> 01:04:09.136
it makes it easy for you to take advantage

01:04:09.136 --> 01:04:11.594
of the broader Firebase platform.

01:04:11.594 --> 01:04:14.649
For example, experimenting
with different custom models

01:04:14.649 --> 01:04:16.461
using A/B testing,

01:04:16.461 --> 01:04:19.898
or storing your image label
into Cloud Firestore,

01:04:19.898 --> 01:04:22.575
or understanding the impact
on user engagement

01:04:22.575 --> 01:04:24.900
with Google Analytics.

01:04:24.900 --> 01:04:26.855
We are so excited about the possibilities

01:04:26.855 --> 01:04:28.521
that machine learning unlocks.

01:04:28.521 --> 01:04:30.387
Whether it's to supercharge your growth

01:04:30.387 --> 01:04:32.715
or build amazing user experiences,

01:04:32.715 --> 01:04:34.634
we want to harness Google's advances

01:04:34.634 --> 01:04:37.454
to help you you build and grow your apps.

01:04:37.454 --> 01:04:39.901
And with that, I'd like
to hand it over to Yufeng,

01:04:39.901 --> 01:04:42.083
to talk more about Cloud AI.

01:04:42.083 --> 01:04:43.144
Thank you.

01:04:43.144 --> 01:04:44.392
(applause)

01:04:44.392 --> 01:04:47.942
♪ (music) ♪

01:04:56.394 --> 01:04:58.124
Thank you, Francis.

01:04:58.124 --> 01:04:59.046
Hi, everyone.

01:04:59.046 --> 01:05:02.081
My name is Yufeng Guo,
and I'm a Developer Advocate

01:05:02.081 --> 01:05:03.835
here at Google Cloud.

01:05:03.835 --> 01:05:06.197
Over the past four years
that I've been at Google

01:05:06.197 --> 01:05:10.256
machine learning has taken on
a greater and greater prominence

01:05:10.256 --> 01:05:15.072
in both our internal usage
as well as in our external offerings.

01:05:15.072 --> 01:05:18.074
Today I want to provide you
with a user's guide

01:05:18.074 --> 01:05:21.385
to thinking about machine learning problems.

01:05:21.385 --> 01:05:24.257
We'll talk about how to find
the right tools for your task

01:05:24.257 --> 01:05:25.924
depending on your needs,

01:05:25.924 --> 01:05:29.017
and we'll discuss four groups
of tools and workflows

01:05:29.017 --> 01:05:33.227
that can be used to drive insights
to build your applications:

01:05:33.227 --> 01:05:36.696
machine learning APIs, data processing,

01:05:36.696 --> 01:05:39.667
model-building, and scaling up.

01:05:39.667 --> 01:05:41.261
Let's start with APIs.

01:05:41.261 --> 01:05:43.255
Say you have some video or sound data

01:05:43.255 --> 01:05:46.173
that you want to annotate
with labels and transcripts.

01:05:46.173 --> 01:05:50.013
Historically, this would have been
a challenging and manual task.

01:05:50.013 --> 01:05:53.624
Video Intelligence API allows you
to turn a video

01:05:53.624 --> 01:05:57.662
into a set of labels and scenes
to build understanding,

01:05:57.662 --> 01:06:01.768
while the Speech API
can turn sound into text.

01:06:01.768 --> 01:06:05.091
This means you can now focus
on doing something useful

01:06:05.091 --> 01:06:07.534
with your newly tagged audio and video files

01:06:07.534 --> 01:06:12.046
instead of getting stuck
on how to label and transcribe it all.

01:06:12.046 --> 01:06:15.718
Additionally, there is the Video API,
the Natural Language API,

01:06:15.718 --> 01:06:17.449
and the Translate API,

01:06:17.449 --> 01:06:21.692
which handle images, text,
in the many languages of the world,

01:06:21.692 --> 01:06:25.300
enabling you to easily transform
and augment your existing data.

01:06:25.300 --> 01:06:27.399
Together, you can think of these five APIs

01:06:27.399 --> 01:06:30.582
as simple building blocks
for your application.

01:06:30.582 --> 01:06:32.472
They're literally just REST APIs

01:06:32.472 --> 01:06:34.679
that you can call and get JSON back.

01:06:34.679 --> 01:06:36.563
Now, if these pre-trained APIs

01:06:36.563 --> 01:06:39.491
don't give quite enough detail for you,

01:06:39.491 --> 01:06:43.050
you can customize them
to produce state-of-the-art models,

01:06:43.050 --> 01:06:47.070
using AutoML for vision,
natural language, and translation.

01:06:47.070 --> 01:06:51.923
All you need to do is provide the data
for your specific use case,

01:06:51.923 --> 01:06:55.736
and AutoML will train up
a custom API endpoint

01:06:55.736 --> 01:06:58.196
just for your particular interest.

01:06:58.196 --> 01:07:01.141
So that's the story
of the machine learning APIs--

01:07:01.141 --> 01:07:03.996
easy to use, and simple to customize.

01:07:03.996 --> 01:07:06.109
But what about other forms of data?

01:07:06.109 --> 01:07:09.022
Let's say you've got
a data scientist at your company,

01:07:09.022 --> 01:07:12.695
or perhaps you are
<i>the</i> data scientist at your company,

01:07:12.821 --> 01:07:16.144
and this data scientist,
she is chomping at the bit

01:07:16.144 --> 01:07:19.530
to get her hands on
as many datasets as possible

01:07:19.530 --> 01:07:23.017
to find interesting relationships
that might exist.

01:07:23.017 --> 01:07:26.707
But, alas, your data is big and it's messy.

01:07:26.707 --> 01:07:29.548
It's stored in a variety
of formats and places.

01:07:29.548 --> 01:07:32.899
How can we use machine learning
to get some meaningful outcomes

01:07:32.899 --> 01:07:35.832
out of this, well, <i>pile</i> of data?

01:07:35.832 --> 01:07:39.649
First, I want to clarify
that machine learning is not magic,

01:07:39.649 --> 01:07:41.738
but, rather, it essentially boils down

01:07:41.738 --> 01:07:45.221
to using many examples to make predictions,

01:07:45.221 --> 01:07:47.074
or at least <i>try</i> to make predictions.

01:07:47.074 --> 01:07:48.706
And so a large part of the work

01:07:48.706 --> 01:07:51.717
is around getting the data organized.

01:07:51.717 --> 01:07:54.508
And because data is often stored
in a variety of places,

01:07:54.508 --> 01:07:56.422
for good reason I'm sure,

01:07:56.422 --> 01:07:59.914
gathering it together, where possible,
is a good first step,

01:07:59.914 --> 01:08:02.295
before then trying to see and get a sense

01:08:02.295 --> 01:08:04.973
of just what it is you're dealing with.

01:08:04.973 --> 01:08:07.047
And how might you do that on GCP?

01:08:07.047 --> 01:08:09.177
Perhaps you explore it in BigQuery,

01:08:09.177 --> 01:08:11.368
run some preprocessing
on Dataflow or Dataproc,

01:08:11.371 --> 01:08:14.143
and take a peek at the results
in a Jupyter notebook.

01:08:14.143 --> 01:08:17.201
Or perhaps you run the data through Dataprep

01:08:17.201 --> 01:08:19.116
and output the results to Cloud Storage

01:08:19.116 --> 01:08:22.118
and save some metadata to Cloud SQL.

01:08:22.118 --> 01:08:23.486
However you approach it,

01:08:23.486 --> 01:08:26.718
careful management
of your data is <i>the</i> key factor

01:08:26.718 --> 01:08:29.734
in being able to do
any kind of meaningful analysis

01:08:29.734 --> 01:08:32.458
and, of course, machine learning.

01:08:32.458 --> 01:08:37.146
So be a good steward to your data,
and it will reward you.

01:08:37.540 --> 01:08:41.718
Okay, so let's say we've got all our data
set up and ready to go.

01:08:41.718 --> 01:08:45.973
It's been turned inside out, upside down,
and arranged just right.

01:08:45.973 --> 01:08:48.536
What's the next step
in our data scientist's journey?

01:08:48.908 --> 01:08:52.031
It's time for her to make some models.

01:08:52.031 --> 01:08:54.407
If she's already got a dataset
stored in BigQuery,

01:08:54.407 --> 01:08:56.556
thanks to all that work
in getting the data prepared,

01:08:56.556 --> 01:08:59.098
it may be worth running some,
say, linear regressions

01:08:59.098 --> 01:09:02.086
directly in SQL, using BigQuery ML.

01:09:02.086 --> 01:09:04.113
And, for a more sophisticated approach,

01:09:04.113 --> 01:09:05.973
she can look to TensorFlow,

01:09:05.973 --> 01:09:08.444
Google's popular
open source deep learning library.

01:09:08.444 --> 01:09:10.666
It's flexible and extensible,
but, best of all,

01:09:10.666 --> 01:09:12.660
runs on all manner of platforms,

01:09:12.660 --> 01:09:16.674
from CPUs and GPUs as well as mobile and web.

01:09:17.075 --> 01:09:19.181
Initially, she'll most likely run TensorFlow

01:09:19.181 --> 01:09:22.193
on her local environment,
say in a Jupyter notebook.

01:09:22.193 --> 01:09:25.945
Maybe she samples a subset
of the larger dataset from the cloud

01:09:25.945 --> 01:09:27.718
and builds a few different models

01:09:27.718 --> 01:09:29.967
to try out different ideas she has.

01:09:29.967 --> 01:09:33.090
Running TensorFlow models
against a subset of the data

01:09:33.090 --> 01:09:34.389
is all well and good,

01:09:34.389 --> 01:09:37.021
but then when it comes time
to train some models

01:09:37.021 --> 01:09:39.487
on the full real dataset,

01:09:39.487 --> 01:09:41.461
running everything on her local laptop

01:09:41.461 --> 01:09:43.810
is probably not so realistic.

01:09:43.810 --> 01:09:46.316
Just getting the data downloaded
would take a while.

01:09:46.316 --> 01:09:49.472
So if we can't bring the data
down to the models,

01:09:49.472 --> 01:09:51.911
let's bring the models to the data,

01:09:51.911 --> 01:09:54.131
by running machine learning in the cloud.

01:09:54.131 --> 01:09:56.379
Imagine being able
to train a model in the cloud

01:09:56.379 --> 01:09:58.675
by just sending up your TensorFlow code

01:09:58.675 --> 01:10:00.650
and virtually nothing else.

01:10:00.650 --> 01:10:04.517
That's basically what training a model
on Cloud ML Engine is like.

01:10:04.517 --> 01:10:07.963
You can train a TensorFlow model, at scale,

01:10:07.963 --> 01:10:11.349
without needing to configure machines
or install libraries.

01:10:11.349 --> 01:10:13.714
You just don't have
to fiddle with that stuff.

01:10:13.714 --> 01:10:16.817
Now this has three
very important implications.

01:10:16.817 --> 01:10:21.080
First, it means you can kick off
multiple training jobs <i>in parallel</i>

01:10:21.080 --> 01:10:24.837
allowing you to try out different approaches
with different datasets.

01:10:24.837 --> 01:10:27.923
And then, well, you can just go get a coffee.

01:10:27.923 --> 01:10:31.946
Second, Cloud ML Engine features
a hyperparameter tuning service

01:10:31.946 --> 01:10:34.751
that runs in parallel
and uses Bayesian optimization

01:10:34.751 --> 01:10:37.885
to try different variants of the same model

01:10:37.885 --> 01:10:40.817
and find the one
that has the best performance.

01:10:40.817 --> 01:10:44.636
And, third, running TensorFlow Training
on Cloud ML Engine

01:10:44.636 --> 01:10:48.567
gives you access not only to CPUs
and a whole lot of GPUs,

01:10:48.567 --> 01:10:49.975
but also TPUs,

01:10:49.975 --> 01:10:54.114
Google's custom accelerator chip
built for deep learning.

01:10:55.168 --> 01:10:57.280
Now once your model is done training,

01:10:57.280 --> 01:10:59.482
it's time to serve predictions.

01:10:59.482 --> 01:11:01.089
And, as most of you know,

01:11:01.089 --> 01:11:04.484
standing up a production server
is no easy feat.

01:11:04.484 --> 01:11:06.915
But with Cloud ML Engine
there are just three steps.

01:11:06.915 --> 01:11:08.270
Are you ready?

01:11:08.270 --> 01:11:10.806
Step one--Provide a name
for this version of your model.

01:11:10.806 --> 01:11:13.870
It can be whatever you want.
Let's call ours <i>version_awesome</i>

01:11:13.870 --> 01:11:17.477
Step two--Provide a location
where the exported model file lives.

01:11:17.477 --> 01:11:19.891
And Step three--Click <i>Save</i>.

01:11:19.891 --> 01:11:23.258
Okay, so we did our three steps.
What did that get us?

01:11:23.258 --> 01:11:27.158
We've actually already got ourselves
an autoscaling REST API

01:11:27.158 --> 01:11:30.419
that can be called
from any internet-connected device

01:11:30.419 --> 01:11:31.803
on the planet.

01:11:32.463 --> 01:11:34.132
And if you thought that's neat,

01:11:34.132 --> 01:11:37.145
wait until you find out you can also
run your existing models

01:11:37.145 --> 01:11:39.443
that are using, say, Scikit-learn and XGBoost

01:11:39.443 --> 01:11:41.831
on Cloud ML Engine as well.

01:11:41.831 --> 01:11:43.534
So you can have just one workflow

01:11:43.534 --> 01:11:46.916
to train and deploy models to the cloud,

01:11:46.916 --> 01:11:49.759
regardless of whether it's using
traditional approaches

01:11:49.759 --> 01:11:51.932
or deep learning approaches.

01:11:52.761 --> 01:11:54.560
Machine learning, it's not easy.

01:11:54.560 --> 01:11:57.704
But when you take our suite
of cloud-native, serverless tools

01:11:57.704 --> 01:12:00.250
to ingest, transform, and manage your data,

01:12:00.250 --> 01:12:03.156
and then combine it
with TensorFlow and Cloud ML Engine,

01:12:03.156 --> 01:12:06.224
you'll be well equipped
to journey into the next generation

01:12:06.224 --> 01:12:08.063
of intelligent applications.

01:12:08.423 --> 01:12:11.957
And if you're ready to dive deeper
into machine learning and data science,

01:12:11.957 --> 01:12:14.309
check out <i>Cloud AI Adventures</i> on YouTube,

01:12:14.309 --> 01:12:17.743
which explores the art, science,
and tools of machine learning.

01:12:17.743 --> 01:12:21.269
You can catch it at <i>YouTube/AI Adventures</i>.

01:12:22.871 --> 01:12:24.916
But I know videos aren't for everyone,

01:12:24.916 --> 01:12:27.829
so here to tell you more about
where you can find a community of learners

01:12:27.829 --> 01:12:30.315
who are supporting each other
to grow in their skills

01:12:30.315 --> 01:12:32.050
and share in their knowledge

01:12:32.050 --> 01:12:35.354
is Anthony Goldbloom, CEO of Kaggle.

01:12:35.354 --> 01:12:37.307
(applause)

01:12:37.307 --> 01:12:41.970
