01:12:48.191 --> 01:12:50.003
So thanks, Yufeng.

01:12:50.003 --> 01:12:52.605
I'm Anthony Goldbloom, CEO of Kaggle.

01:12:52.605 --> 01:12:54.448
And it's really nice
to be back on the Next stage.

01:12:54.448 --> 01:12:56.691
It was actually on this stage last year
that we announced

01:12:56.691 --> 01:12:59.002
that Kaggle was joining Google.

01:12:59.002 --> 01:13:01.080
So for those of you who don't know Kaggle,

01:13:01.080 --> 01:13:03.607
we are the world's largest
machine learning community.

01:13:03.607 --> 01:13:06.401
We're going to pass 2 million members
in the next couple of weeks.

01:13:06.401 --> 01:13:07.899
Now, at the beginning of the show

01:13:07.899 --> 01:13:10.420
Adam asked how many of you
had deployed a machine learning model

01:13:10.420 --> 01:13:12.513
and it seems like not very many.

01:13:12.513 --> 01:13:14.724
We want to change that.

01:13:14.724 --> 01:13:17.757
We want to be a part
of your entire machine learning journey.

01:13:17.757 --> 01:13:21.093
Our community ranges
from people just learning Python

01:13:21.093 --> 01:13:24.311
through to the researchers
behind deep neural networks.

01:13:24.311 --> 01:13:27.163
Kaggle rankings are probably
the most widely recognized

01:13:27.163 --> 01:13:29.472
machine learning credential at this point

01:13:29.472 --> 01:13:31.329
and so we've seen many in our community

01:13:31.329 --> 01:13:35.315
going from being total newbies
to having elite AI roles.

01:13:35.315 --> 01:13:38.768
So the Kaggle community started out
with machine learning competitions,

01:13:38.768 --> 01:13:40.713
so a lot of you will think of Kaggle

01:13:40.713 --> 01:13:42.399
as machine learning competitions,

01:13:42.399 --> 01:13:44.401
but we've expanded well beyond that.

01:13:44.401 --> 01:13:46.164
If you're a software engineer

01:13:46.164 --> 01:13:49.038
you go to Stack Overflow
for your question answering

01:13:49.038 --> 01:13:51.134
and you go to GitHub for code collaboration.

01:13:51.134 --> 01:13:55.205
What Kaggle's trying to do
is be the one place you go for your...

01:13:55.205 --> 01:13:57.843
... As a member
of the machine learning community,

01:13:57.843 --> 01:14:00.470
we're giving you
the one destination you need to go.

01:14:00.470 --> 01:14:04.500
So in addition to competitions,
we offer several other things.

01:14:04.500 --> 01:14:08.258
We offer Kaggle Learn,
which is short-form AI education.

01:14:08.258 --> 01:14:10.772
We offer Kaggle Kernels,
which is a cloud-based workbench

01:14:10.772 --> 01:14:12.513
for data science and machine learning.

01:14:12.513 --> 01:14:15.486
And we have a Datasets platform
where we have almost 10,000 datasets

01:14:15.486 --> 01:14:18.215
that have been shared among our community.

01:14:18.215 --> 01:14:21.457
And so I'm going to give you a tour
of each of these parts of Kaggle,

01:14:21.457 --> 01:14:24.347
and help to show you how they fit

01:14:24.347 --> 01:14:26.708
into your machine learning journey.

01:14:26.708 --> 01:14:29.956
So if you're brand new
to AI and machine learning

01:14:29.956 --> 01:14:32.018
I suggest starting with Kaggle Learn.

01:14:32.018 --> 01:14:35.555
As I mentioned, this is
very short-form AI education.

01:14:35.555 --> 01:14:37.324
So if you go to Udacity,

01:14:37.324 --> 01:14:40.006
the main machine learning course
takes six months.

01:14:40.006 --> 01:14:43.846
Kaggle Learn has you training your first
deep neural network in 30 minutes.

01:14:43.846 --> 01:14:46.051
Now, we're not teaching you
everything there is to know

01:14:46.051 --> 01:14:48.009
about deep neural networks in 30 minutes,

01:14:48.009 --> 01:14:50.715
but what we are doing is
we're giving you enough of a basis

01:14:50.715 --> 01:14:53.538
to get going on your own projects.

01:14:53.538 --> 01:14:56.202
So we have a cloud-based environment
called Kaggle Kernels,

01:14:56.202 --> 01:14:58.762
and you can use it with Kaggle Learn,
but actually you can use it

01:14:58.762 --> 01:15:00.693
for most machine learning projects now.

01:15:00.693 --> 01:15:04.030
Kaggle Kernels allows you to avoid the pain

01:15:04.030 --> 01:15:05.907
of setting up your own environment.

01:15:05.907 --> 01:15:07.855
So we give you a cloud-based environment

01:15:07.855 --> 01:15:10.173
in the browser,

01:15:10.173 --> 01:15:12.129
that will give you machine learning

01:15:12.129 --> 01:15:15.529
with most of the major packages
that you could want.

01:15:15.529 --> 01:15:16.973
We are strong believers

01:15:16.973 --> 01:15:19.010
that the future of data science
and machine learning

01:15:19.010 --> 01:15:22.469
is going to be done in the cloud,
in an environment like Kaggle Kernels--

01:15:22.469 --> 01:15:24.420
hopefully Kaggle Kernels, for you.

01:15:24.420 --> 01:15:26.625
And the reason being is 
that machine learning workloads

01:15:26.625 --> 01:15:27.962
are very bursty.

01:15:27.962 --> 01:15:29.831
One minute you're exploring your data,

01:15:29.831 --> 01:15:32.950
the next minute you're training
a very large deep neural network.

01:15:32.950 --> 01:15:35.389
So the cloud is perfect for such workloads.

01:15:35.815 --> 01:15:38.880
So with Kaggle Kernels you choose

01:15:38.880 --> 01:15:42.151
between a Jupyter notebook interface
or a normal IDE.

01:15:42.151 --> 01:15:45.700
You choose between Python and R,
so those are the two main

01:15:45.700 --> 01:15:47.691
data science machine learning languages.

01:15:47.691 --> 01:15:49.373
And by default we give you a CPU,

01:15:49.373 --> 01:15:53.418
but you can also have access to a GPU
if you want more compute.

01:15:53.418 --> 01:15:58.399
You can see your datasets
and see previews of your dataset,

01:15:58.399 --> 01:16:02.776
and the code executes
on Google Cloud servers.

01:16:03.786 --> 01:16:06.685
So you get six hours of compute
per session at no cost.

01:16:06.685 --> 01:16:11.412
So actually this will cover the majority
of your machine learning workloads.

01:16:11.712 --> 01:16:15.065
Okay. So through Kaggle Learn
you've learned an algorithm.

01:16:15.065 --> 01:16:17.665
Through Kaggle Kernels
you have an environment set up.

01:16:17.665 --> 01:16:19.529
Now you're ready
to start working on a project,

01:16:19.529 --> 01:16:23.013
and we have two projects at Kaggle
that you can work on.

01:16:23.013 --> 01:16:27.673
We have Kaggle Competitions
and we have our Datasets platform.

01:16:27.673 --> 01:16:31.615
Now the way competitions work
is a company will post a problem,

01:16:31.615 --> 01:16:33.526
and data scientists
and machine learners compete

01:16:33.526 --> 01:16:36.190
to build the best algorithm on that problem.

01:16:36.190 --> 01:16:39.123
So, at any point in time,
we're running 10-15 competitions.

01:16:39.123 --> 01:16:42.179
Right now, if you come to the site,
you'll see we're working with Zillow

01:16:42.179 --> 01:16:44.623
to improve the Zestimate
Home Valuation algorithm.

01:16:44.623 --> 01:16:47.444
And we're also working with CERN
to build an algorithm

01:16:47.444 --> 01:16:49.421
that better detects particle collisions

01:16:49.421 --> 01:16:51.392
in the Large Hadron Collider.

01:16:51.392 --> 01:16:55.114
I like showing those two use cases
because they give you a sense of the breadth

01:16:55.114 --> 01:16:59.334
of machine learning and how it's applica...
Those are <i>totally</i> different use cases, right?

01:16:59.334 --> 01:17:00.714
And it's applicable to both.

01:17:00.714 --> 01:17:03.241
Now, during the competition--
you can see on the second cube--

01:17:03.241 --> 01:17:04.971
you can see a live leaderboard

01:17:04.971 --> 01:17:07.807
which shows you how you're performing
relative to others.

01:17:07.807 --> 01:17:10.197
So it also shows you,
if you're not in first place,

01:17:10.197 --> 01:17:12.328
it shows you how much better
it's possible to do.

01:17:12.338 --> 01:17:15.621
So if you're scoring 50%,
and somebody else is scoring 90%,

01:17:15.621 --> 01:17:18.050
they're found something
that you haven't found.

01:17:18.050 --> 01:17:20.680
Very rewarding, also,
while you're participating

01:17:20.680 --> 01:17:22.904
in the competitions we have forums,

01:17:22.904 --> 01:17:25.866
where people are sharing
techniques and ideas.

01:17:25.866 --> 01:17:28.252
and so you never run out
of ideas of things to try.

01:17:28.252 --> 01:17:31.508
All you need to do is read the forums
if you're running out of ideas.

01:17:31.508 --> 01:17:34.799
And, actually, at the end of the competition
very often what we'll see

01:17:34.799 --> 01:17:37.367
is we'll see the top performers will share

01:17:37.367 --> 01:17:40.130
the results of their model in the forums.

01:17:40.130 --> 01:17:41.855
Sorry, the approach they took.

01:17:41.855 --> 01:17:44.987
So you kind of get to learn
what the right answer is.

01:17:44.987 --> 01:17:46.028
Alright.

01:17:46.028 --> 01:17:49.161
So our Datasets platform is the other place
you can go and do projects,

01:17:49.161 --> 01:17:52.822
and this is really good
for more freeform projects.

01:17:52.822 --> 01:17:55.772
Our community has shared
almost 10,000 datasets at this point.

01:17:55.772 --> 01:17:59.211
So even if you have your own dataset
that you want to work on,

01:17:59.211 --> 01:18:03.304
rather than working in isolation
on your local machine,

01:18:03.304 --> 01:18:05.261
why not bring it to Kaggle?

01:18:05.261 --> 01:18:07.419
And then others who are also
interested in that dataset

01:18:07.419 --> 01:18:08.967
will work on it with you, right?

01:18:08.967 --> 01:18:10.968
You'll attract a small community of people

01:18:10.968 --> 01:18:13.532
who are interested
in the problem you're working on.

01:18:13.532 --> 01:18:14.544
On the flip side,

01:18:14.544 --> 01:18:17.243
if you <i>don't</i> have a dataset
that you're itching to work with,

01:18:17.243 --> 01:18:19.449
as I said, we have almost 10,000 datasets.

01:18:19.449 --> 01:18:22.236
And the range of topics
is kind of breathtaking.

01:18:22.236 --> 01:18:23.280
I'll show you in a minute.

01:18:23.280 --> 01:18:28.806
But there is almost certainly
a dataset that meets your interests.

01:18:28.806 --> 01:18:32.653
So I'm going to show you what it looks like
to work on our Datasets platform.

01:18:32.653 --> 01:18:36.895
Here I'm browsing the datasets
that others have uploaded,

01:18:36.895 --> 01:18:40.065
and I'll find a dataset on avocado prices.

01:18:40.705 --> 01:18:44.252
I like avocado toast,
so I figure I'll jump into this one.

01:18:44.722 --> 01:18:46.655
And rather than starting from scratch,

01:18:46.655 --> 01:18:50.255
I start looking at what others
have done on this dataset.

01:18:50.255 --> 01:18:52.408
And I notice that somebody
called Samuel Belko

01:18:52.408 --> 01:18:54.999
has already written a forecasting model.

01:18:54.999 --> 01:18:57.889
And his forecasting model has done
exactly the kinds of things

01:18:57.889 --> 01:18:59.275
that I would want to do.

01:18:59.275 --> 01:19:02.523
I'm scrolling quickly, but you can see
he's done some exploration,

01:19:02.523 --> 01:19:03.910
he's doing some charting,

01:19:03.910 --> 01:19:08.515
then he uses quite a well-known
time series forecasting package

01:19:08.515 --> 01:19:10.181
in order to make a prediction.

01:19:10.181 --> 01:19:12.489
So I hit the <i>Fork</i> button,
and that gives me...

01:19:12.489 --> 01:19:16.060
When I hit <i>Fork</i>, what's happening
behind the scenes is I'm forking the code,

01:19:16.060 --> 01:19:18.240
the Docker container,
and the connection to the data.

01:19:18.243 --> 01:19:22.343
So I get his code, in an environment
that will run it immediately.

01:19:22.343 --> 01:19:26.529
And, as you can see, I'm scrolling through,
I have exactly the same code that he has.

01:19:26.529 --> 01:19:28.697
And then when I start executing the cells,

01:19:28.697 --> 01:19:32.822
I'm getting the same results that he gets.

01:19:32.822 --> 01:19:34.964
So, as I dig into his results,

01:19:34.964 --> 01:19:38.815
I notice that avocado prices
rose pretty dramatically in 2017,

01:19:38.815 --> 01:19:41.904
and I want to build a model
that sort of fits that period nicely,

01:19:41.904 --> 01:19:43.462
and explains that period nicely.

01:19:43.462 --> 01:19:47.144
So I go in, and I add a dataset,
and I add a dataset with Starbucks locations,

01:19:47.144 --> 01:19:49.093
which at first glance sounds kind of odd.

01:19:49.093 --> 01:19:51.372
What do avocado prices
and Starbucks locations

01:19:51.372 --> 01:19:52.676
have to do with each other?

01:19:52.676 --> 01:19:55.639
Well, it turns out that Starbucks
rolled out an avocado spread,

01:19:55.639 --> 01:19:57.540
nationwide, in 2017,

01:19:57.540 --> 01:20:00.965
and so when I add in the dataset
on Starbucks locations

01:20:00.965 --> 01:20:06.544
it helps me to evaluate the impact

01:20:06.544 --> 01:20:09.579
of the additional Starbucks' demand
on avocado prices.

01:20:09.582 --> 01:20:12.797
So you can see how I can go
and extend Samuel's model

01:20:12.797 --> 01:20:14.403
by adding in an extra dataset,

01:20:14.403 --> 01:20:16.409
and adding that dataset into the model.

01:20:16.409 --> 01:20:20.872
If I wanted to grab weather data, as well--
almost certainly we have a weather dataset

01:20:20.872 --> 01:20:25.012
for some of the main avocado-growing regions.

01:20:25.012 --> 01:20:26.966
So maybe that had an impact on supply.

01:20:28.256 --> 01:20:29.940
So hopefully that gives you a sense

01:20:29.940 --> 01:20:33.368
for how many learning opportunities,

01:20:33.368 --> 01:20:34.776
how many interesting datasets,

01:20:34.776 --> 01:20:37.932
how much there is to do
on the Kaggle Datasets platform.

01:20:37.932 --> 01:20:39.944
So, as I said at the beginning,

01:20:39.944 --> 01:20:42.527
Kaggle wants to be a part
of your whole machine learning journey.

01:20:42.527 --> 01:20:43.923
And so over the coming year,

01:20:43.923 --> 01:20:47.112
you'll start to see us add in
more commercial-grade features.

01:20:47.112 --> 01:20:50.257
You'll start to see us allow you
to connect your Kaggle accounts

01:20:50.257 --> 01:20:51.906
to your GCP accounts.

01:20:51.906 --> 01:20:55.734
You'll be able to access
the datasets you have in BigQuery.

01:20:55.734 --> 01:20:58.483
You'll be able to deploy your models
to Cloud ML Engine.

01:20:58.483 --> 01:21:01.790
And so you'll get the content
that has been shared

01:21:01.790 --> 01:21:03.333
with the Kaggle community,

01:21:03.333 --> 01:21:05.855
plus the power of GCP's data analytics

01:21:05.855 --> 01:21:07.459
and machine learning services.

01:21:07.459 --> 01:21:11.637
So for those of you who haven't yet deployed
your first machine learning model,

01:21:11.637 --> 01:21:14.789
who aren't yet a part of our community,
we'd love you to sign up.

01:21:15.335 --> 01:21:18.027
We want to help accelerate
your machine learning journey.

01:21:19.037 --> 01:21:21.672
So Kaggle is one of the great communities
around GCP,

01:21:21.672 --> 01:21:23.544
but next on stage we have Sam O'Keefe,

01:21:23.544 --> 01:21:25.658
who heads up Google Cloud for Startups.

01:21:25.658 --> 01:21:28.525
And Sam is going to talk about
some of the other communities

01:21:28.525 --> 01:21:29.676
we have around GCP.

01:21:29.676 --> 01:21:31.090
Thank you.

01:21:31.310 --> 01:21:32.648
(applause)

01:21:32.648 --> 01:21:35.459
♪ (music) ♪

01:21:42.018 --> 01:21:43.551
Thanks, Anthony.

01:21:43.551 --> 01:21:47.955
Kaggle and GCP's machine learning tools
are just a few examples

01:21:47.955 --> 01:21:50.857
of the way that Google's values manifest

01:21:50.857 --> 01:21:52.967
in the tools and technology we create.

01:21:53.458 --> 01:21:57.434
I hope you're heard by now, but just in case,

01:21:57.434 --> 01:21:59.655
we are committed to openness,

01:21:59.655 --> 01:22:03.301
and we want all of you,
the community, involved.

01:22:03.301 --> 01:22:06.236
Next up, we'll hear
from one of our close partners,

01:22:06.236 --> 01:22:09.627
who shares those values--
Unity Technologies.

01:22:09.627 --> 01:22:11.735
We recently announced that GCP

01:22:11.735 --> 01:22:14.527
would be powering the Unity platform.

01:22:15.157 --> 01:22:16.737
With this seamless integration,

01:22:16.737 --> 01:22:19.808
game developers get to focus
on what they love--

01:22:19.808 --> 01:22:24.201
being creative and building
immersive experiences for their users,

01:22:24.201 --> 01:22:26.870
while we take care of the infrastructure.

01:22:26.870 --> 01:22:28.426
Let's take a peek.

01:22:30.940 --> 01:22:35.011
♪ (music) ♪

01:23:24.879 --> 01:23:28.488
(applause)

01:23:29.486 --> 01:23:32.774
Here to tell us more,
please welcome Suhail Dutta,

01:23:32.774 --> 01:23:35.607
VP of Cloud Services for Unity.

01:23:36.925 --> 01:23:41.281
♪ (music) ♪

01:23:50.921 --> 01:23:54.702
At Unity, we believe
the world's a better place

01:23:54.702 --> 01:23:56.614
with more creators in it.

01:23:57.678 --> 01:24:01.421
So whether we're helping Steven Spielberg
shoot <i>Ready Player One</i>

01:24:01.421 --> 01:24:03.120
on a virtual set;

01:24:03.944 --> 01:24:07.589
or working with NASA
on visualizing the Mars Rover;

01:24:08.175 --> 01:24:13.039
empowering Lexus to design
and market the LC500,

01:24:14.452 --> 01:24:19.583
or providing Ludia with the tools
to create <i>Jurassic World Alive</i>,

01:24:20.477 --> 01:24:26.561
Unity is the most widely used,
real-time, 3D development platform.

01:24:27.161 --> 01:24:30.614
And we're empowering millions of creators

01:24:30.614 --> 01:24:32.641
to achieve their aspirations.

01:24:33.785 --> 01:24:36.897
At Unity, we live by three principles:

01:24:37.598 --> 01:24:39.646
democratize development,

01:24:39.646 --> 01:24:41.688
solve hard problems,

01:24:41.688 --> 01:24:43.432
enable success.

01:24:44.575 --> 01:24:46.919
One of the hardest problems in gaming

01:24:47.737 --> 01:24:50.724
is building what we call <i>connected games</i>.

01:24:51.740 --> 01:24:55.430
So games that help their players connect
and play with each other

01:24:56.067 --> 01:24:58.815
are easily the most
influential games out there.

01:24:59.340 --> 01:25:01.870
They're the most played, the most watched,

01:25:01.870 --> 01:25:03.937
and the most successful.

01:25:04.809 --> 01:25:09.103
In fact, 90% of the top grossing apps
on Google Play

01:25:09.103 --> 01:25:11.059
are connected games.

01:25:12.781 --> 01:25:16.044
But creating them is hard.

01:25:17.072 --> 01:25:19.476
Building an experience
that delights your users

01:25:19.476 --> 01:25:23.045
all across the world
requires a lot of infrastructure--

01:25:23.872 --> 01:25:26.688
the time and expertise to manage it,

01:25:27.219 --> 01:25:29.446
and 24/7 support.

01:25:30.543 --> 01:25:33.572
At Unity, we want to change that.
And so does Google.

01:25:34.272 --> 01:25:37.827
Last month, we announced a strategic alliance

01:25:38.732 --> 01:25:41.869
to help simplify the development
of connected games.

01:25:41.869 --> 01:25:44.597
And we're doing this
keeping two tenets in mind:

01:25:45.681 --> 01:25:47.873
scaleable and approachable.

01:25:49.076 --> 01:25:53.152
Scaleable, so you can create your game
the right way from day one,

01:25:53.152 --> 01:25:56.457
whether you have one player or a million.

01:25:57.421 --> 01:26:01.708
And we do this with efficient,
high-performance networking

01:26:02.662 --> 01:26:05.536
and enterprise-grade server hosting.

01:26:06.482 --> 01:26:08.800
We also use our tools and services

01:26:08.800 --> 01:26:12.497
to provide a seamless integration
between Unity and Google Cloud,

01:26:12.497 --> 01:26:16.175
so you can get started quickly
and focus on what you do best--

01:26:16.175 --> 01:26:18.671
creating great experiences.

01:26:20.659 --> 01:26:22.922
Our first collaboration,

01:26:22.922 --> 01:26:25.782
a service that helps match players into games

01:26:25.782 --> 01:26:28.863
based on an open source
matchmaking project,

01:26:29.539 --> 01:26:31.091
is coming out later this summer.

01:26:31.091 --> 01:26:32.866
It's called Open Match.

01:26:34.756 --> 01:26:37.073
Not only do we believe

01:26:37.073 --> 01:26:41.927
that Google Cloud
is the right cloud for our customers,

01:26:41.927 --> 01:26:45.974
but we're also migrating our infrastructure
over to Google Cloud.

01:26:47.583 --> 01:26:50.660
Together, we're making it easier

01:26:50.660 --> 01:26:54.850
for you to build and operate
your connected experiences.

01:26:55.690 --> 01:26:58.470
We can't wait to see what you create.

01:26:58.470 --> 01:26:59.705
Thank you.

01:26:59.705 --> 01:27:03.846
(applause)

01:27:05.055 --> 01:27:08.311
Unity's developer community
continues to demonstrate

01:27:08.311 --> 01:27:09.791
that with the right tools,

01:27:09.791 --> 01:27:11.725
small teams can build products

01:27:11.725 --> 01:27:14.365
that reach and are enjoyed by millions.

01:27:14.365 --> 01:27:17.364
I have the unique opportunity
to witness this every day

01:27:17.364 --> 01:27:19.714
as part of Google Cloud for Startups.

01:27:19.714 --> 01:27:21.715
At Google Cloud for Startups,

01:27:21.715 --> 01:27:23.912
we help developers from all over the world

01:27:23.912 --> 01:27:27.238
get their businesses up and running on GCP.

01:27:27.238 --> 01:27:30.363
We do this by providing access--

01:27:30.363 --> 01:27:32.916
access to content, to help you learn;

01:27:32.916 --> 01:27:36.034
and technical mentors,
for when you get stuck;

01:27:36.034 --> 01:27:39.404
access to Google products
at a discounted rate,

01:27:39.404 --> 01:27:42.299
so they're affordable
when you're in your garage phase;

01:27:42.299 --> 01:27:45.674
and, most importantly,
access to a community,

01:27:45.674 --> 01:27:48.318
a community of peers facing and solving

01:27:48.318 --> 01:27:52.522
the same technical
and non-technical challenges that you are.

01:27:52.522 --> 01:27:54.954
In keeping with our theme of openness,

01:27:54.954 --> 01:27:58.428
Google Cloud for Startups
truly is for everyone--

01:27:58.428 --> 01:28:01.912
from two developers
working on their first MVP,

01:28:01.912 --> 01:28:05.558
to a team that just found
product/market fit and is figuring out,

01:28:05.558 --> 01:28:08.365
"How the <i>heck</i> are we going
to keep this thing going?"--

01:28:08.365 --> 01:28:10.501
Google wants to be there.

01:28:10.501 --> 01:28:13.131
You can get started today
by going to our website,

01:28:13.131 --> 01:28:15.363
and we'd love to meet you.

01:28:15.363 --> 01:28:18.971
In the past year, thousands of startups
have made the decision

01:28:18.971 --> 01:28:20.661
to build with Google.

01:28:20.661 --> 01:28:23.507
You saw a few of these startups
in the video earlier,

01:28:23.507 --> 01:28:25.266
but they're really doing everything--

01:28:25.266 --> 01:28:28.537
they're leveraging machine learning
to structure healthcare data;

01:28:28.537 --> 01:28:31.977
using IoT devices
to create factories of the future;

01:28:31.977 --> 01:28:34.089
and, if you're here in the Bay Area,

01:28:34.089 --> 01:28:37.023
they're putting <i>everything</i> on the blockchain.

01:28:37.023 --> 01:28:41.818
Today I'm excited to have two startups here,
to share their story,

01:28:41.818 --> 01:28:44.713
and show us all what's possible
with Google Cloud.

01:28:45.399 --> 01:28:47.964
As we heard in Aja's talk earlier,

01:28:47.964 --> 01:28:51.792
JavaScript is one
of <i>the</i> most popular languages today.

01:28:51.792 --> 01:28:55.255
So our first startup
<i>might</i> be familiar to many of you.

01:28:56.172 --> 01:28:59.979
npm provides the largest
software registry in the world,

01:28:59.979 --> 01:29:04.945
enabling over 11 million JS developers
to build web applications.

01:29:04.945 --> 01:29:08.712
Please join me in welcoming
CTO, CJ Silverio,

01:29:08.712 --> 01:29:12.379
as she tells us how a team
of three engineers gets it done.

01:29:12.840 --> 01:29:16.994
♪ (music) ♪

01:29:25.403 --> 01:29:29.624
How many of you here work
for a company that has a website?

01:29:29.624 --> 01:29:30.898
Website? Anyone?

01:29:30.898 --> 01:29:32.826
Yeah, your company has a website? Great.

01:29:32.826 --> 01:29:34.834
You are my user.

01:29:34.834 --> 01:29:38.670
Hi, I'm CJ Silverio, CTO of npm, Inc.

01:29:38.670 --> 01:29:43.008
Now, npm powers the world's largest registry

01:29:43.008 --> 01:29:44.536
of open source software.

01:29:44.536 --> 01:29:46.082
If you have a website,

01:29:46.082 --> 01:29:48.839
use <i>any</i> modern front-end framework,

01:29:48.839 --> 01:29:51.654
your developers type <i>npm install</i>.

01:29:51.654 --> 01:29:55.509
You use us because we are
how the world shares JavaScript.

01:29:56.547 --> 01:30:00.388
I'm here today because our team
took our global registry

01:30:00.388 --> 01:30:03.449
and created an enterprise-ready version of it

01:30:03.449 --> 01:30:07.029
to help developers at big companies
use open source code at work.

01:30:07.029 --> 01:30:10.050
We did using Google Kubernetes Engine.

01:30:10.500 --> 01:30:14.985
npm's registry
is a complex distributed system.

01:30:14.985 --> 01:30:17.974
It stores data in three different databases

01:30:17.974 --> 01:30:19.822
and a Cloud Object store,

01:30:19.822 --> 01:30:22.350
and it has the usual thicket of microservices

01:30:22.350 --> 01:30:24.639
shuttling that data around.

01:30:24.639 --> 01:30:29.444
It was designed to make it easy
for us to scale the registry

01:30:29.444 --> 01:30:31.433
to a billion downloads a day,

01:30:31.433 --> 01:30:35.730
supporting our 11 million users, all of you.

01:30:35.730 --> 01:30:37.968
It's massive, and it's reliable,

01:30:37.968 --> 01:30:40.804
to the point that people don't even
think about what happens

01:30:40.804 --> 01:30:43.307
when they type <i>npm install</i>.

01:30:43.307 --> 01:30:45.277
Here's the twist.

01:30:45.277 --> 01:30:48.005
We had enterprise companies asking us

01:30:48.005 --> 01:30:51.078
how they could have
their own private npm registry--

01:30:51.078 --> 01:30:53.244
all the features of the public registry,

01:30:53.244 --> 01:30:56.888
but meeting their greater need
for control over their source code.

01:30:56.888 --> 01:31:00.789
The challenge was to take
a super redundant architecture

01:31:00.789 --> 01:31:03.553
that sprawls across hundreds
of cloud instances,

01:31:03.553 --> 01:31:07.848
and package it into something
easy to install, manage, and operate.

01:31:08.722 --> 01:31:11.544
Now that's a lot of complexity
that has to be reduced

01:31:11.544 --> 01:31:14.121
and delivered to a single customer.

01:31:14.838 --> 01:31:17.502
Did I mention that npm is a <i>small</i> company?

01:31:17.502 --> 01:31:18.957
Because we are.

01:31:18.957 --> 01:31:20.835
And we already have a full-time job

01:31:20.835 --> 01:31:24.298
running the world's largest registry
of open source software.

01:31:26.697 --> 01:31:30.469
Containerization was clearly
the way for us to go with this,

01:31:30.469 --> 01:31:34.288
and for the first version of this product,
we containerized.

01:31:34.697 --> 01:31:37.274
But we did that with a hand-tuned,

01:31:37.274 --> 01:31:39.482
single-size container deployment.

01:31:39.482 --> 01:31:43.360
And we ended up with something
that was hard to scale and hard to update.

01:31:44.634 --> 01:31:46.694
When we looked at doing another take on it,

01:31:46.694 --> 01:31:48.782
we knew we had to use Kubernetes.

01:31:49.719 --> 01:31:54.713
Now, just saying, "Yeah, Kubernetes,"
is not an answer.

01:31:55.152 --> 01:31:56.652
You have to install Kubernetes,

01:31:56.652 --> 01:31:59.144
you have to manage it,
and you have to operate it.

01:31:59.431 --> 01:32:02.445
And then you have to scale that to demand

01:32:02.445 --> 01:32:04.110
as we bring in new customers,

01:32:04.110 --> 01:32:07.548
and as our customers grow
and add users and packages.

01:32:07.548 --> 01:32:11.641
So the idea of building
all this infrastructure ourselves

01:32:11.641 --> 01:32:13.101
was daunting.

01:32:13.733 --> 01:32:18.073
Google Kubernetes Engine, though,
had exactly the API we needed.

01:32:18.073 --> 01:32:20.546
It may sound like a line
fed to me by marketing,

01:32:20.546 --> 01:32:22.327
but it's literally true.

01:32:23.229 --> 01:32:26.409
Give me a cluster.
Here are my compute memory requirements.

01:32:26.409 --> 01:32:27.496
Go!

01:32:27.496 --> 01:32:30.048
GKE gives me a cluster.

01:32:30.048 --> 01:32:33.848
Now I need a place
to store my customers' data

01:32:33.848 --> 01:32:35.428
and keep it backed up.

01:32:35.428 --> 01:32:38.016
Google Cloud Storage is right there.

01:32:39.057 --> 01:32:42.369
Now, I need isolation,

01:32:42.369 --> 01:32:46.083
so I can manage and bill
each of our customers separately.

01:32:46.083 --> 01:32:49.753
We use GCP's Projects
within our Google Cloud organization,

01:32:49.753 --> 01:32:52.668
to keep our customers' resources isolated.

01:32:53.107 --> 01:32:58.208
So, no joke, a few months later,
a single team of <i>three developers</i>

01:32:58.208 --> 01:33:01.546
built and shipped an all new npm Enterprise,

01:33:01.546 --> 01:33:04.104
and it's serving customers <i>now</i>.

01:33:04.370 --> 01:33:07.711
You can run the exact software
that the npm registry runs,

01:33:07.711 --> 01:33:09.545
with your own authentication,

01:33:09.545 --> 01:33:11.645
in a single-tenant environment,

01:33:11.645 --> 01:33:13.272
all on Google Cloud.

01:33:13.977 --> 01:33:16.170
We can provision a cluster, scale it,

01:33:16.170 --> 01:33:17.701
keep our customers upgraded,

01:33:17.701 --> 01:33:20.689
and <i>I didn't need to hire a staff</i>

01:33:20.689 --> 01:33:22.863
that's expert in Kubernetes, to do it.

01:33:23.375 --> 01:33:26.865
Enterprise customers get all the features
the public registry gets

01:33:26.865 --> 01:33:28.763
at the same time it gets them.

01:33:29.308 --> 01:33:33.515
Using GKE shaved a year
off of our development time

01:33:33.515 --> 01:33:37.318
and let us focus on what we do best--
building amazing software.

01:33:39.168 --> 01:33:43.938
npm's mission is to reduce developer friction

01:33:43.938 --> 01:33:46.503
for <i>every single</i>
software developer in the world.

01:33:46.503 --> 01:33:49.777
GKE reduced friction <i>for us,</i>

01:33:49.777 --> 01:33:52.337
so that we can deliver
this amazing thing <i>to you,</i>

01:33:52.337 --> 01:33:55.379
so that <i>you</i> can build
amazing things yourselves.

01:33:55.379 --> 01:33:57.880
Sam, we're big fans of GKE,

01:33:57.880 --> 01:34:00.075
and I'm <i>super</i> excited for what's ahead.

01:34:00.075 --> 01:34:01.193
(Sam) I am, too.

01:34:01.193 --> 01:34:03.821
And congratulations on everything
you've accomplished so far.

01:34:03.821 --> 01:34:05.106
Thank you, CJ.

01:34:05.106 --> 01:34:08.803
(applause)

01:34:10.610 --> 01:34:13.464
I don't think it'll be a surprise
to anyone in this audience

01:34:13.464 --> 01:34:16.533
when I say that data
is critical to machine learning.

01:34:16.533 --> 01:34:20.684
Much like how Google's Fei-Fei Li
created ImageNet

01:34:20.684 --> 01:34:23.220
sparked a frenzy of advancement in the field,

01:34:23.220 --> 01:34:27.445
our next startup is building
a massive dataset of their own.

01:34:27.445 --> 01:34:30.111
This time, that dataset consists

01:34:30.111 --> 01:34:34.383
of millions of microscopic images
of human cells.

01:34:34.383 --> 01:34:37.480
Armed with this data, and Google Cloud,

01:34:37.480 --> 01:34:39.736
Recursion Pharmaceuticals is on a mission

01:34:39.736 --> 01:34:42.554
to redefine the drug discovery process

01:34:42.554 --> 01:34:46.333
and bring life-saving drugs to market faster.

01:34:46.333 --> 01:34:48.803
Please join me in welcoming Lina Nilsson,

01:34:48.803 --> 01:34:51.943
Senior Director of Data Science
at Recursion Pharmaceuticals,

01:34:51.943 --> 01:34:53.141
to tell us more.

01:34:53.669 --> 01:34:57.714
♪ (music) ♪

01:35:06.512 --> 01:35:11.078
We are decoding biology
in order to find new medical treatments.

01:35:11.078 --> 01:35:14.931
I'm Lina Nilsson, and I am
incredibly excited to be here today

01:35:14.931 --> 01:35:17.461
to share with you Recursion's vision

01:35:17.461 --> 01:35:20.133
for the future of drug discovery.

01:35:20.133 --> 01:35:23.082
The number of new drugs
approved each year by the FDA

01:35:23.082 --> 01:35:26.506
has been essentially stagnant
since the 1980s,

01:35:26.506 --> 01:35:29.361
and the development cost
of each of those few drugs

01:35:29.361 --> 01:35:33.984
has increased tenfold,
to well over two billion dollars.

01:35:34.605 --> 01:35:36.300
Contrast this to the world of tech,

01:35:36.300 --> 01:35:38.782
where costs are coming down all the time,

01:35:38.782 --> 01:35:41.858
and where the smartphone
in my pocket has more compute

01:35:41.858 --> 01:35:44.698
than a 1980s supercomputer.

01:35:44.698 --> 01:35:47.779
So why has drug discovery not
followed this same pattern?

01:35:48.520 --> 01:35:51.484
The traditional scientific workflow
is sequential.

01:35:51.484 --> 01:35:56.636
Researchers work on one disease
and one hypothesis at a time.

01:35:56.636 --> 01:36:01.710
This works, but the rate
of finding new treatments is slow.

01:36:01.710 --> 01:36:04.951
At Recursion, we're transforming
this old process

01:36:04.951 --> 01:36:08.038
into a new formula, where we test dozens,

01:36:08.038 --> 01:36:10.959
or even hundreds of diseases at once.

01:36:11.410 --> 01:36:14.170
And instead of testing
one hypothesis at a time

01:36:14.170 --> 01:36:16.024
across that dataset,

01:36:16.024 --> 01:36:19.541
we're able to be open
to millions of possible outcomes,

01:36:19.545 --> 01:36:21.303
in an unbiased way.

01:36:21.809 --> 01:36:24.238
And this new formula gives us the ability

01:36:24.238 --> 01:36:28.150
to dramatically speed up
the discovery of new drugs.

01:36:29.017 --> 01:36:31.324
The key to all of this is a combination of:

01:36:31.329 --> 01:36:36.159
one, large-scale biology
on our automated robotics platform,

01:36:36.159 --> 01:36:39.511
then two, prediction
and inference on the cloud,

01:36:39.511 --> 01:36:44.112
and, finally, rigorous
biology validation in the lab

01:36:44.112 --> 01:36:46.737
to complete the drug discovery process.

01:36:47.142 --> 01:36:49.893
So let's dig in a bit,
and I'll show you a bit more

01:36:49.909 --> 01:36:51.380
about how this all works.

01:36:51.885 --> 01:36:54.221
The first step is automated robotics.

01:36:54.657 --> 01:36:56.903
Recursion has built an automated lab,

01:36:56.903 --> 01:37:02.351
full of robots, that run about
100,000 experiments every week.

01:37:02.351 --> 01:37:07.190
This gives us two million biology images
to work with <i>every</i> week.

01:37:07.674 --> 01:37:09.371
By the end of this year,

01:37:09.371 --> 01:37:11.728
we'll have the capability to generate

01:37:11.728 --> 01:37:15.555
nearly 1 PB of image data every month.

01:37:16.069 --> 01:37:18.438
This dwarfs ImageNet.

01:37:19.069 --> 01:37:22.047
And these biology images
are then streamed to Google Cloud

01:37:22.047 --> 01:37:24.746
and are run through
our data processing platform,

01:37:24.746 --> 01:37:27.128
which leverages Google Kubernetes Engine,

01:37:27.128 --> 01:37:30.441
BigQuery, and, of course,
Google Cloud Storage.

01:37:31.340 --> 01:37:35.139
And this is what those microscope images
of human cells look like.

01:37:35.139 --> 01:37:38.115
Each image you see
shows a different cell type,

01:37:38.115 --> 01:37:40.423
exposed to different diseases,

01:37:40.423 --> 01:37:42.809
and, of course,
different potential treatments.

01:37:43.979 --> 01:37:46.079
But generating millions of images alone,

01:37:46.079 --> 01:37:49.180
that's not really going to help us
reach our ambitious goal.

01:37:49.764 --> 01:37:52.270
It is only transformative when we layer on

01:37:52.270 --> 01:37:54.852
machine learning analytics on the cloud

01:37:54.852 --> 01:37:58.154
in order to discover
important patterns in the data.

01:37:58.905 --> 01:38:01.775
Using preemptable virtual machines,
for example,

01:38:01.775 --> 01:38:03.368
our workloads can spike,

01:38:03.368 --> 01:38:04.916
to do all this image processing

01:38:04.916 --> 01:38:07.813
and deep learning inference using TensorFlow.

01:38:07.813 --> 01:38:10.034
And that allows us to iterate rapidly

01:38:10.034 --> 01:38:12.850
and to develop new, powerful
machine learning models

01:38:12.850 --> 01:38:14.997
in a really cost-effective way.

01:38:15.445 --> 01:38:18.959
So let me give you one
really powerful example of a model.

01:38:18.959 --> 01:38:20.747
So here we built a unified,

01:38:20.747 --> 01:38:23.526
high-dimensional representation latent space

01:38:23.526 --> 01:38:26.596
to help us identify the most likely drugs

01:38:26.596 --> 01:38:28.401
to work for a given disease,

01:38:28.401 --> 01:38:31.228
before we even run that test in the lab.

01:38:31.228 --> 01:38:35.398
So here I'm showing just a small subset
of this latent space data,

01:38:35.398 --> 01:38:37.374
in a simplified 3D graphic,

01:38:37.374 --> 01:38:40.332
so essentially taking thousands of dimensions

01:38:40.332 --> 01:38:43.889
and turning that into something
that's visually interpretable by humans.

01:38:44.664 --> 01:38:47.702
The red dots indicate
the results of adding disease

01:38:47.702 --> 01:38:49.522
to human cells in our lab,

01:38:49.522 --> 01:38:53.397
and the blue dots represent
a separate addition of drugs

01:38:53.397 --> 01:38:54.986
to cells in the lab.

01:38:54.986 --> 01:38:56.675
The closer two dots are,

01:38:56.675 --> 01:39:01.418
the more similar their effects
on human cells, and vice versa.

01:39:01.418 --> 01:39:04.312
So it turns out that drugs tend to cluster

01:39:04.312 --> 01:39:06.791
on the opposite side of the latent space

01:39:06.791 --> 01:39:08.683
from the drugs that they work on,

01:39:08.683 --> 01:39:12.304
so the diseases that they can treat.

01:39:12.304 --> 01:39:13.986
So while not a perfect predictor,

01:39:13.986 --> 01:39:16.097
this means that we have a data pattern

01:39:16.097 --> 01:39:20.024
to help us identify drugs
that are likely to be effective.

01:39:20.024 --> 01:39:24.356
Or, to put it another way,
these blue dot drugs you see here

01:39:24.356 --> 01:39:25.864
are likely to be effective

01:39:25.864 --> 01:39:28.483
against the red dot disease
on the opposite side.

01:39:29.558 --> 01:39:31.199
This is really powerful,

01:39:31.199 --> 01:39:33.361
because it means that for the first time

01:39:33.361 --> 01:39:36.167
we do not have to test every drug

01:39:36.167 --> 01:39:39.435
against every disease in the laboratory.

01:39:39.435 --> 01:39:41.965
We can predict, already on the cloud,

01:39:41.965 --> 01:39:44.613
which drugs are likely to work.

01:39:44.613 --> 01:39:48.379
And this is the type of approach
that saves our scientists tons of time

01:39:48.379 --> 01:39:50.283
and tons of resources.

01:39:50.583 --> 01:39:53.541
And it's also an example
of the kind of capability

01:39:53.541 --> 01:39:55.457
Recursion is building out

01:39:55.457 --> 01:39:59.760
in order to be able to exponentially
increase the efficiency in drug discovery.

01:40:00.803 --> 01:40:03.495
And that brings us
to our final step, step three.

01:40:03.495 --> 01:40:05.767
Our team of biologists validate and follow up

01:40:05.767 --> 01:40:07.535
on all of our results.

01:40:07.996 --> 01:40:10.524
Robotics, machine learning models,

01:40:10.524 --> 01:40:14.066
and human biologists
then work hand in hand, together,

01:40:14.066 --> 01:40:17.274
to speed up all the steps
of the drug discovery pipeline

01:40:17.274 --> 01:40:19.445
from <i>in vitro</i> to <i>in vivo</i>,

01:40:19.445 --> 01:40:21.956
and then, eventually, human medical trials.

01:40:22.763 --> 01:40:24.480
In fact, using this new formula,

01:40:24.480 --> 01:40:27.872
Recursion already has one drug
cleared by the FDA

01:40:27.872 --> 01:40:29.349
for human medical trials,

01:40:29.349 --> 01:40:32.467
and we have a pipeline
of dozens of other drugs behind it,

01:40:32.467 --> 01:40:34.489
in just five short years.

01:40:34.489 --> 01:40:36.212
This is a tremendous volume,

01:40:36.212 --> 01:40:39.559
not just for a small startup
like for Recursion,

01:40:39.559 --> 01:40:41.829
but for any biopharma company.

01:40:42.876 --> 01:40:45.520
We are on a journey to decode biology

01:40:45.520 --> 01:40:49.549
and to find cures
for today's untreatable diseases.

01:40:49.549 --> 01:40:51.812
I hope you are inspired to join us.

01:40:52.142 --> 01:40:53.406
Thank you.

01:40:53.406 --> 01:40:54.824
(applause)

01:40:54.827 --> 01:40:59.084
♪ (music) ♪

01:40:59.382 --> 01:41:01.316
Alright! Hey!

01:41:01.726 --> 01:41:04.728
Alright! Was that a fun time, or what?

01:41:05.101 --> 01:41:06.131
Yeah!

01:41:06.538 --> 01:41:08.104
So I want to recap.

01:41:08.104 --> 01:41:10.527
Our promise to you, our commitment to you,

01:41:10.527 --> 01:41:13.426
the first is around innovation,
the second is around openness,

01:41:13.426 --> 01:41:16.075
and the third is about
being part of a community.

01:41:16.075 --> 01:41:19.770
Now I've got something to announce
that I want you to pay attention to.

01:41:20.113 --> 01:41:22.276
I'm incredibly excited to announce

01:41:22.276 --> 01:41:24.903
that our community is running DevFests--

01:41:24.903 --> 01:41:26.730
community-organized developer events--

01:41:26.730 --> 01:41:30.859
in 500 cities, starting basically
in a couple of weeks.

01:41:30.859 --> 01:41:32.678
So I hope you all find a local one.

01:41:32.678 --> 01:41:35.015
Get plugged into that local community!

01:41:35.015 --> 01:41:37.872
I want to thank all of you.
Thanks for your participation.

01:41:37.872 --> 01:41:39.970
Thanks for your feedback and your challenge.

01:41:39.970 --> 01:41:41.600
Now we're going to do something...

01:41:41.600 --> 01:41:43.826
... Another thing I want to tell you
that we're going to do now,

01:41:43.826 --> 01:41:45.586
is we're going to officially announce

01:41:45.586 --> 01:41:48.438
a save-the-date for the next Next:

01:41:48.438 --> 01:41:51.878
April 9-11 in San Francisco, right here.

01:41:51.878 --> 01:41:55.491
♪ (music) ♪

